# Master Textbook Structured Repository for all 38 Chapters
MASTER_TEXTBOOK_DATA = {('DL', 0): {'chapter_number': 5, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Denormalized Exponent Bias Trap', 'warning': 'For denormalized numbers (E = 0), the exponent power is 2^(-126), NOT 2^(-127)! The hidden bit is 0, not 1.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>The Three Signed Number Formats for n Bits:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nRepresentation    │ Range for n bits                    │ Zero Representation(s)\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nSign-Magnitude    │ -(2ⁿ⁻¹ - 1) to +(2ⁿ⁻¹ - 1)          │ Two zeros (+0 and -0)\n1's Complement    │ -(2ⁿ⁻¹ - 1) to +(2ⁿ⁻¹ - 1)          │ Two zeros (+0 and -0)\n2's Complement    │ -2ⁿ⁻¹ to +(2ⁿ⁻¹ - 1)               │ UNIQUE ZERO (00...0)\n</div>\n\n• <b>Why Modern Computers Use 2's Complement Exclusively:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Unique Zero:</b> Only one representation for zero (no +0 and -0 ambiguity).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>One Extra Negative Number:</b> For 8 bits, range is -128 to +127 (128 cannot be represented in +ve!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Simplified Hardware:</b> Subtraction <code>A - B</code> is performed using existing adder hardware as <code>A + (2's complement of B)</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Shortcut for Finding 2's Complement in Binary:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Scan bits from Right to Left. Keep all bits identical up to and including the FIRST '1'. Invert all remaining bits to the left!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Sign extension in 2's complement: To convert an n-bit signed number to m-bit (m > n), replicate the MSB (sign bit) to the left! E.g. 4-bit '1101' (-3) becomes 8-bit '11111101' (-3).", 'number': '5.1', 'title': "Signed Number Systems & 2's Complement"}, {'content': '• <b>What is Overflow?</b> When the result of adding two signed numbers exceeds the representable range of n bits.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>The Fundamental Rule of Overflow:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Adding two POSITIVE numbers results in a NEGATIVE number ⇒ <b>OVERFLOW!</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Adding two NEGATIVE numbers results in a POSITIVE number ⇒ <b>OVERFLOW!</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Adding numbers of OPPOSITE signs CAN NEVER cause overflow!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Hardware Detection Condition:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>V = Cᵢₙ ⊕ Cₒᵤₜ</code> of the Most Significant Bit (MSB).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;If Carry-into-MSB ≠ Carry-out-of-MSB, an overflow has occurred!', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Carry flag (C) is used for unsigned arithmetic. Overflow flag (V) is used for signed 2's complement arithmetic! Never confuse the two.", 'number': '5.2', 'title': "Arithmetic Overflow in 2's Complement"}, {'content': "• <b>Single Precision (32-Bit) Format Layout:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>\n[ Sign: 1 bit ] [ Exponent (E): 8 bits ] [ Mantissa / Fraction (M): 23 bits ]\nBit 31          Bits 30-23               Bits 22-0\n</div>\n\n• <b>Decoding Formula for Normalized Numbers (1 ≤ E ≤ 254):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Value = (-1)ˢ × (1.M)₂ × 2^(E - 127)</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Sign bit (S):</b> 0 = Positive, 1 = Negative.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Bias = 127:</b> The stored exponent E is biased: <code>E = Actual Exponent + 127</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Hidden Bit:</b> The leading '1.' before the mantissa is NOT stored in memory to save 1 bit of precision!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Special IEEE 754 Representations:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>E = 0, M = 0</code>: Represents Zero (±0 depending on S).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>E = 255, M = 0</code>: Represents Infinity (±∞).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>E = 255, M ≠ 0</code>: Represents <b>NaN</b> (Not a Number, e.g. 0/0).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>E = 0, M ≠ 0</code>: Denormalized numbers: <code>(-1)ˢ × (0.M)₂ × 2^(-126)</code>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Double Precision (64-bit): 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits (Bias = 1023), 52 mantissa bits. Smallest positive normalized number in 32-bit is 1.0 × 2^(-126).', 'number': '5.3', 'title': 'IEEE 754 Floating-Point Standard (32-Bit Single Precision)'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'IEEE 754 is just binary scientific notation: instead of writing 6.02 × 10²³, the computer writes 1.011 × 2⁴. The sign, power, and digits are packed into 32 bits.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Scientific Notation on Pocket Calculators'}], 'title': 'Number Representation & Floating Point (IEEE 754)', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Hexadecimal IEEE 754 representation is 0xC11C0000.', 'problem': 'Convert the decimal number -9.75 into 32-bit IEEE 754 single-precision hexadecimal representation.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Sign bit S = 1 (since number is negative).', 'Step 2: Convert magnitude 9.75 to binary: 9 = 1001, 0.75 = 0.11. So 9.75 = 1001.11₂.', 'Step 3: Normalize to scientific binary: 1001.11₂ = 1.00111₂ × 2³.', 'Step 4: Actual exponent = 3. Biased exponent E = 3 + 127 = 130 = 10000010₂.', 'Step 5: Mantissa bits (drop hidden 1): 00111000000000000000000 (pad with zeros to 23 bits).', 'Step 6: Combine: [1] [10000010] [00111000000000000000000] = 1100 0001 0001 1100 0000 0000 0000 0000.', 'Step 7: Group into hex digits: C11C0000₁₆.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Converting Decimal to IEEE 754 Format'}]}, ('DL', 1): {'chapter_number': 6, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'The False Minimal Expression Trap', 'warning': 'A function can have multiple distinct minimal SOP expressions with the same number of terms and literals. Do not assume there is only one valid answer.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Universal Logic Gates (NAND and NOR):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any Boolean function can be implemented using ONLY NAND or ONLY NOR gates without requiring any other gate!<br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nFunction / Gate to Implement │ Minimum NAND Gates Needed │ Minimum NOR Gates Needed\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nNOT (Inverter)               │ 1                         │ 1\nAND Gate                     │ 2                         │ 3\nOR Gate                      │ 3                         │ 2\nXOR Gate                     │ 4                         │ 5\nXNOR Gate                    │ 5                         │ 4\n</div>\n\n• <b>The Consensus Theorem (GATE High-Yield Simplification Shortcut):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>AB + A'C + BC = AB + A'C</code> (The term BC is redundant consensus!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>How to spot Consensus in 5 seconds:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Three terms, each with 2 variables.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. One variable appears complemented (A and A').<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3. The term containing the remaining two variables (BC) is COMPLETELY REDUNDANT and can be deleted!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Dual of Consensus Theorem: (A + B)(A' + C)(B + C) = (A + B)(A' + C). Keep an eye out for both SOP and POS forms in GATE questions.", 'number': '6.1', 'title': 'Universal Logic Gates & Consensus Theorem'}, {'content': "• <b>Gray Code Ordering in K-Maps:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- K-map row/column labels MUST use Gray code: <b>00, 01, 11, 10</b> (only 1 bit changes between adjacent cells).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Because of wrap-around adjacency, corners and edges are adjacent to each other!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Implicant Terminology (Tested in every GATE paper):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Implicant:</b> Any single 1 or group of 1s (of size 1, 2, 4, 8, ...).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> A rectangle of 1s (and don't cares) that CANNOT be merged into a larger group.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A Prime Implicant that contains <b>at least one '1' that is NOT covered by any other PI</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Minimal SOP:</b> MUST include ALL EPIs + the minimum number of remaining PIs to cover leftover 1s.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Never group don't cares (X) with other don't cares! Only include a don't care in a group if it helps expand a group of 1s to make it larger (reducing literal count).", 'number': '6.2', 'title': 'Karnaugh Maps (K-Maps) & Implicants'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Every transistor on a microchip consumes silicon real estate and power. K-map logic minimization literally reduces the physical size and battery consumption of smartphone processors.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Silicon Chip Area & Cost'}], 'title': 'Boolean Algebra, K-Maps & Tabular Method (Quine-McCluskey)', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': '3 Prime Implicants, and all 3 are Essential Prime Implicants (EPIs).', 'problem': 'For the Boolean function f(A,B,C,D) = ∑m(0, 2, 5, 7, 8, 10, 14, 15), find the number of Prime Implicants and Essential Prime Implicants.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Draw 4-variable K-Map with Gray codes 00, 01, 11, 10 for AB and CD.', 'Step 2: Place 1s at minterms 0, 2, 8, 10 (four corners), 5, 7 (adjacent in row 01), and 14, 15 (adjacent in row 11).', "Step 3: Group four corners (0, 2, 8, 10) ⇒ Term: B'D'.", "Step 4: Group (5, 7) ⇒ Term: A'BD.", 'Step 5: Group (14, 15) ⇒ Term: ABC.', "Step 6: Check EPI status: Minterm 0 is only in B'D' (so B'D' is EPI). Minterm 5 is only in A'BD (so A'BD is EPI). Minterm 14 is only in ABC (so ABC is EPI)."], 'title': 'Worked Example: Finding Prime Implicants and Essential PIs'}]}, ('DL', 2): {'chapter_number': 7, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Select Line Order Inversion Trap', 'warning': 'If S₁ is connected to B and S₀ is connected to A instead of A to S₁ and B to S₀, the row indices flip completely. Always verify which variable is MSB in the select lines.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>What is a Multiplexer?</b> A data selector with 2ⁿ data inputs, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Implementing n-Variable Functions using (2ⁿ⁻¹)-to-1 MUX:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Connect (n - 1) variables to the select lines.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Connect the remaining 1 variable (in forms: 0, 1, V, or V') to the data inputs.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Example:</b> To implement a 3-variable function f(A, B, C) using a 4-to-1 MUX:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Connect A and B to select lines S₁ and S₀.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Compare outputs for each combination of AB: find whether input Iᵢ equals 0, 1, C, or C'.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any n-variable Boolean function can be implemented using a 2ⁿ-to-1 MUX with NO external gates, or using a 2ⁿ⁻¹-to-1 MUX with at most NOT gates!', 'number': '7.1', 'title': 'Multiplexers (MUX) as Universal Function Generators'}, {'content': '• <b>Decoders (n-to-2ⁿ):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Generates all 2ⁿ minterms of the n input variables.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any function f can be implemented using a Decoder + OR gate (for active-high outputs) or Decoder + NAND gate (for active-low outputs).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Priority Encoders:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Resolves simultaneous requests: if multiple inputs are 1, only the highest-priority input is encoded.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Includes a Valid output bit (V = 1 if at least one input is active).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Number of smaller m-to-2ᵐ decoders needed to build an n-to-2ⁿ decoder = 2ⁿ / 2ᵐ.', 'number': '7.2', 'title': 'Decoders, Encoders & Priority Logic'}, {'content': '• <b>Full Adder Equations:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Sum = A ⊕ B ⊕ Cᵢₙ</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Carry = AB + BCᵢₙ + ACᵢₙ</code> (Can be implemented with 2 Half Adders and 1 OR gate).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Carry Lookahead Adder (CLA):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Eliminates ripple carry propagation delay by computing carries in parallel!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Generate bit:</b> <code>Gᵢ = Aᵢ · Bᵢ</code> (Carry generated internally).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Propagate bit:</b> <code>Pᵢ = Aᵢ ⊕ Bᵢ</code> (Carry propagated from previous stage).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Carries: <code>C₁ = G₀ + P₀C₀</code>, <code>C₂ = G₁ + P₁G₀ + P₁P₀C₀</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Total delay of an n-bit CLA is constant O(1), independent of n!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Ripple Carry Adder delay grows linearly O(n). Carry Lookahead Adder delay is independent of n, limited only by gate fan-in constraints.', 'number': '7.3', 'title': 'Adders & Carry Lookahead Architecture'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'A multiplexer is like a railway track junction where multiple incoming train tracks merge into a single line. The select line controls which train gets routed forward.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Railway Track Switcher'}], 'title': 'Combinational Circuits - MUX, Decoders & Arithmetic Circuits', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': "MUX inputs: I₀ = C, I₁ = C, I₂ = C', I₃ = C'.", 'problem': 'Implement f(A,B,C) = ∑m(1, 3, 4, 6) using a 4-to-1 MUX with A and B connected to select lines S₁ and S₀ respectively.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Set up truth table grouped by AB values (00, 01, 10, 11).', 'Step 2: For AB = 00 (minterms 0, 1): m0=0, m1=1 ⇒ When C=0, f=0; when C=1, f=1. So I₀ = C.', 'Step 3: For AB = 01 (minterms 2, 3): m2=0, m3=1 ⇒ When C=0, f=0; when C=1, f=1. So I₁ = C.', "Step 4: For AB = 10 (minterms 4, 5): m4=1, m5=0 ⇒ When C=0, f=1; when C=1, f=0. So I₂ = C'.", "Step 5: For AB = 11 (minterms 6, 7): m6=1, m7=0 ⇒ When C=0, f=1; when C=1, f=0. So I₃ = C'."], 'title': 'Worked Example: Implementing Logic with 4-to-1 MUX'}]}, ('DL', 3): {'chapter_number': 8, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Ring Counter vs Johnson Counter State Count Trap', 'warning': 'For 4 flip-flops: Ring counter has ONLY 4 states, whereas Johnson counter has 2 × 4 = 8 states. A normal binary counter has 2⁴ = 16 states.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Characteristic Equations (Next State Q⁺):</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nFlip-Flop │ Characteristic Equation      │ Forbidden / Invalid State\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nSR        │ Q⁺ = S + R'Q (with SR = 0)     │ S = 1, R = 1\nJK        │ Q⁺ = JQ' + K'Q                 │ None (Toggles when J=K=1)\nD         │ Q⁺ = D                         │ None (Transparent / Delay)\nT         │ Q⁺ = T ⊕ Q = TQ' + T'Q         │ None (Toggles when T=1)\n</div>\n\n• <b>Excitation Tables (Required inputs to transition from Q → Q⁺):</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nQ  Q⁺ │ S   R │ J   K │ D │ T\n──────────────────────────────\n0  0  │ 0   X │ 0   X │ 0 │ 0\n0  1  │ 1   0 │ 1   X │ 1 │ 1\n1  0  │ 0   1 │ X   1 │ 0 │ 1\n1  1  │ X   0 │ X   0 │ 1 │ 0\n</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To convert Flip-Flop A to Flip-Flop B: Write characteristic equation of B, and match it with the excitation table of A.', 'number': '8.1', 'title': 'Flip-Flop Characteristic & Excitation Equations'}, {'content': '• <b>Setup Time (tₛᵤ) & Hold Time (tₕ):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Setup Time:</b> The minimum time the input data MUST remain stable <b>BEFORE</b> the active clock edge.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Hold Time:</b> The minimum time the input data MUST remain stable <b>AFTER</b> the active clock edge.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Violating setup or hold times causes <b>Metastability</b> (unpredictable oscillating output).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Maximum Clock Frequency Formula:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>T_clock ≥ t_cq + t_comb + t_setup</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>f_max = 1 / T_clock_min = 1 / (t_cq + t_comb + t_setup)</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;(where t_cq = clock-to-Q propagation delay, t_comb = combinational logic delay).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Hold time DOES NOT affect maximum clock frequency! Hold time condition is: t_cq + t_comb ≥ t_hold.', 'number': '8.2', 'title': 'Flip-Flop Timing Constraints & Max Frequency'}, {'content': "• <b>Asynchronous (Ripple) vs Synchronous Counters:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Ripple Counter:</b> Output of each flip-flop clocks the next. Simple hardware, but propagation delay accumulates: <code>T_delay = n × t_pd</code>. Max frequency decreases rapidly with n.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Synchronous Counter:</b> All flip-flops share the same clock line. All change simultaneously: <code>T_delay = t_pd + t_comb</code> (much faster!).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Special Shift Register Counters (n Flip-Flops):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Ring Counter:</b> Feedback from Qₙ to D₀. Number of unique states = <b>n</b>. Unused states = 2ⁿ - n.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Johnson (Twisted Ring) Counter:</b> Feedback from Q'ₙ to D₀. Number of unique states = <b>2n</b>. Unused states = 2ⁿ - 2n.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A Mod-N counter counts N states (from 0 to N-1) and requires ⌈log₂ N⌉ flip-flops.', 'number': '8.3', 'title': 'Synchronous & Asynchronous Counters, Shift Registers'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'In a Moore machine, a pedestrian walk sign depends only on the current light cycle (e.g. Red). In a Mealy machine, pressing the crosswalk button can instantly change the sign immediately.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Traffic Light Controller (Mealy vs Moore)'}], 'title': 'Sequential Circuits - Flip-Flops, Counters & State Machines', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Maximum clock frequency is 100 MHz.', 'problem': 'A sequential circuit has flip-flops with clock-to-Q delay of 3 ns, setup time of 2 ns, and hold time of 1 ns. The combinational logic between flip-flops has a maximum delay of 5 ns. Find the maximum clock frequency at which this circuit can operate safely.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate the minimum clock period T_min using: T_min ≥ t_cq + t_comb + t_setup.', 'Step 2: Substitute values: T_min = 3 ns + 5 ns + 2 ns = 10 ns.', 'Step 3: Calculate maximum frequency f_max = 1 / T_min = 1 / (10 × 10⁻⁹ s) = 100 MHz.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Maximum Operating Clock Frequency'}]}, ('EM', 0): {'chapter_number': 1, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'The Vacuous Truth Trap in First-Order Logic', 'warning': "When evaluating ∀x [P(x) → Q(x)] on an empty set or a domain where P(x) is False for all elements, the formula evaluates to TRUE! Many students select False thinking 'no elements satisfy the condition'."}, {'title': 'Anti-Symmetric vs Asymmetric Confusion', 'warning': 'Anti-symmetric allows diagonal elements (a, a) to exist. Asymmetric STRICTLY FORBIDS diagonal elements! Hence, every Asymmetric relation is Anti-symmetric, but not vice versa.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>What is a Proposition? (Basic Definition):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- A proposition is a declarative sentence that is strictly <b>True (T)</b> or strictly <b>False (F)</b>, but never both simultaneously.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples of propositions: <i>'7 is a prime number'</i> (True), <i>'Paris is in Japan'</i> (False).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- NOT propositions: Questions (<i>'What time is it?'</i>), Commands (<i>'Close the door'</i>), or Paradoxes (<i>'This statement is false'</i>).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Master Truth Table for the 5 Logical Connectives:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nP   Q │ ~P (NOT) │ P ∧ Q (AND) │ P ∨ Q (OR) │ P → Q (Implication) │ P ↔ Q (Biconditional)\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nT   T │    F     │      T      │     T      │          T          │          T\nT   F │    F     │      F      │     T      │          F          │          F\nF   T │    T     │      F      │     T      │          T          │          F\nF   F │    T     │      F      │     F      │          T          │          T\n</div>\n\n• <b>Why is (F → T) and (F → F) True? (Vacuous Truth Explained):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Think of an implication <code>P → Q</code> as a conditional contract: <i>'If you score Rank 1 in GATE (P), I will buy you a laptop (Q)'</i>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If you do NOT score Rank 1 (<code>P = False</code>), whatever happens next does NOT break the contract! Whether I give you a laptop or not, the contract was never breached.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Therefore: <b>When P is False, the implication P → Q is ALWAYS True by default!</b><br/><br/>\n\n• <b>The Most Critical Logical Equivalence in GATE:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>(P → Q) ≡ (~P ∨ Q)</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- In 90% of GATE logic questions, you can solve the question in 20 seconds by replacing every <code>P → Q</code> with <code>(~P ∨ Q)</code>!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: P → Q is FALSE only in one single scenario: P is True and Q is False! In all other 3 scenarios, P → Q is strictly TRUE.', 'number': '1.1', 'title': 'Propositions, Truth Values & The 5 Core Connectives'}, {'content': "• <b>Definitions for any Conditional Statement 'If P, then Q' (P → Q):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Contrapositive:</b> <code>~Q → ~P</code> ('If NOT Q, then NOT P').<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Converse:</b> <code>Q → P</code> ('If Q, then P').<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Inverse:</b> <code>~P → ~Q</code> ('If NOT P, then NOT Q').<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Equivalence Relationships (GATE Must-Know):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The Golden Identity:</b> <code>(P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A statement and its Contrapositive ALWAYS have the exact same truth value under all conditions.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>GATE Application:</i> If a mathematical theorem is difficult to prove directly (P → Q), prove its contrapositive (~Q → ~P) instead!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The Converse Trap:</b> <code>(P → Q) ≢ (Q → P)</code>. The Converse is NOT equivalent to the original statement!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The Inverse Relationship:</b> The Inverse (<code>~P → ~Q</code>) is logically equivalent to the Converse (<code>Q → P</code>), but NOT to the original statement.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Original Statement ≡ Contrapositive. Converse ≡ Inverse. A statement is NEVER guaranteed to equal its converse!', 'number': '1.2', 'title': 'Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse'}, {'content': "• <b>The Two Core Quantifiers:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Universal Quantifier (∀):</b> 'For all', 'For every', 'For each'.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>∀x P(x)</code> is True only if P(x) is True for <i>every single element</i> in the domain.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃):</b> 'There exists', 'For at least one', 'Some'.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>∃x P(x)</code> is True if there is <i>at least one element</i> that makes P(x) True.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>De Morgan's Laws for Quantifiers (Negation Rules):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>~[∀x P(x)] ≡ ∃x [~P(x)]</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Intuition:</i> To disprove 'Every student is honest', you only need to find ONE dishonest student!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>~[∃x P(x)] ≡ ∀x [~P(x)]</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Intuition:</i> To disprove 'There exists a flying pig', you must show that ALL pigs cannot fly.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Order of Quantifiers (Commutativity vs Non-Commutativity):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Same quantifiers commute: <code>∀x ∀y P(x,y) ≡ ∀y ∀x P(x,y)</code> and <code>∃x ∃y P(x,y) ≡ ∃y ∃x P(x,y)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Mixed quantifiers DO NOT commute!</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>∀x ∃y Loves(x, y)</code> means: 'Every person loves someone' (Everyone has someone they love).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>∃y ∀x Loves(x, y)</code> means: 'There is a single celebrity whom everybody loves'!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Note: <code>∃y ∀x P(x, y) ⇒ ∀x ∃y P(x, y)</code>, but the converse is FALSE!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When translating English to FOL: Universal quantifier (∀) almost always pairs with Implication (→): ∀x [Student(x) → Hardworking(x)]. Existential quantifier (∃) pairs with Conjunction (∧): ∃x [Student(x) ∧ Hardworking(x)]. Using ∃ with → is a notorious trap because of vacuous truth!', 'number': '1.3', 'title': 'First-Order Logic: Quantifiers (∀ vs ∃) & Translation'}, {'content': "• <b>Power Set:</b> For a set A with n elements (|A| = n), the power set P(A) contains <b>2ⁿ</b> subsets.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Relations on a Set A (|A| = n):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;A binary relation R on A is a subset of A × A. Total possible relations = <b>2^(n²)</b>.<br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nRelation Type   │ Definition Condition                  │ Total Possible Count Formula\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nReflexive       │ ∀a ∈ A: (a, a) ∈ R                    │ 2^(n² - n)\nIrreflexive     │ ∀a ∈ A: (a, a) ∉ R                    │ 2^(n² - n)\nSymmetric       │ (a, b) ∈ R ⇒ (b, a) ∈ R              │ 2^[n(n + 1) / 2]\nAnti-Symmetric  │ (a, b) ∈ R and (b, a) ∈ R ⇒ a = b     │ 2ⁿ × 3^[n(n - 1) / 2]\nAsymmetric      │ (a, b) ∈ R ⇒ (b, a) ∉ R              │ 3^[n(n - 1) / 2]\n</div>\n\n• <b>Equivalence Relations:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- A relation is an Equivalence Relation if it is <b>Reflexive, Symmetric, and Transitive</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Every equivalence relation partitions the set into disjoint <b>Equivalence Classes</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Total number of equivalence relations on a set of size n equals the <b>Bell Number B(n)</b>: B(1)=1, B(2)=2, B(3)=5, B(4)=15, B(5)=52.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Function Classifications (Mapping f: A → B, |A| = m, |B| = n):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Injective (One-to-One):</b> Distinct inputs map to distinct outputs. Possible only if m ≤ n. Count = <code>ⁿPₘ = n! / (n - m)!</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Surjective (Onto):</b> Every element in B is covered. Possible only if m ≥ n. Count = <code>∑ (-1)ᵏ ⁿCₖ (n - k)ᵐ</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Bijective (One-to-One & Onto):</b> Possible only if m = n. Count = <code>n!</code>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A relation can be BOTH Symmetric and Anti-symmetric (e.g. Identity relation R = {(1,1), (2,2)}). But a relation CANNOT be both Reflexive and Irreflexive (except for empty set).', 'number': '1.4', 'title': 'Set Theory, Relations & Function Properties'}, {'content': '• <b>What is a POSET? (Partially Ordered Set):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- A relation R on set A is a Partial Order if it is: <b>Reflexive, Anti-symmetric, and Transitive</b> (RAT).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Visualized using a <b>Hasse Diagram</b> (transitive and reflexive edges omitted, smaller elements drawn below larger ones).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Key Elements in a POSET:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Maximal Element:</b> An element x such that no element y exists with x < y (top of branch). A POSET can have <i>multiple</i> maximal elements!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Greatest Element (Maximum):</b> An element g such that ∀x, x ≤ g. If it exists, it is <b>UNIQUE</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Minimal Element:</b> An element with no element below it. Can have multiple.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Least Element (Minimum):</b> An element l such that ∀x, l ≤ x. If it exists, it is <b>UNIQUE</b>!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>What is a Lattice?</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- A POSET (L, ≤) is called a <b>Lattice</b> if every pair of elements {a, b} has:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. A unique <b>Least Upper Bound (LUB / Join: a ∨ b)</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. A unique <b>Greatest Lower Bound (GLB / Meet: a ∧ b)</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Bounded Lattice:</b> Has a greatest element (1) and least element (0). Every finite lattice is bounded!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Distributive Lattice:</b> Satisfies distributive laws: <code>a ∧ (b ∨ c) = (a ∧ b) ∨ (a ∧ c)</code>. A lattice is distributive iff it does NOT contain the pentagon (N₅) or diamond (M₃) sublattices!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In any finite lattice, the Greatest element and Least element MUST exist and be unique! If two top elements have no single common ceiling, it is NOT a lattice.', 'number': '1.5', 'title': 'Partial Orders (POSETs) and Lattices'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "An employment contract states: 'If you complete the milestone (P), you get a bonus (Q)'. If you do not finish the milestone (P = False), the company has not broken its promise regardless of whether it gives a bonus or not.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Employment Contract & Vacuous Truth'}], 'title': 'Discrete Mathematics - Mathematical Logic & Set Theory', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'The formula is a TAUTOLOGY (Valid statement).', 'problem': 'Simplify the compound propositional formula: [ (P → Q) ∧ (Q → R) ] → (P → R). Determine whether it is a Tautology, Contradiction, or Contingency.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify that this formula represents the classical Hypothetical Syllogism rule of inference.', 'Step 2: Rewrite using the implication equivalence A → B ≡ ~A ∨ B. Let antecedent be H = (P → Q) ∧ (Q → R). The statement becomes ~H ∨ (P → R).', 'Step 3: Analyze truth values. Assume the entire formula is False. For an implication A → B to be False, antecedent A must be True and consequent B must be False.', 'Step 4: If consequent (P → R) is False, we must have P = True and R = False.', 'Step 5: Substitute P = True and R = False into antecedent H: H = (True → Q) ∧ (Q → False) = Q ∧ ~Q = False.', 'Step 6: But H was assumed to be True! We reached a contradiction (False = True). Hence, it is impossible for the formula to ever be False.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Logic Equivalence & Implication Simplification'}, {'answer': '11,664 Anti-symmetric relations.', 'problem': 'Let set A = {1, 2, 3, 4}. How many total relations on A are Anti-symmetric?', 'steps': ['Step 1: The size of set A is n = 4. The Cartesian product A × A has n² = 16 pairs.', 'Step 2: Partition the 16 pairs into two groups: (a) Diagonal pairs (x, x) where there are n = 4 pairs, and (b) Off-diagonal symmetric pairs {(x, y), (y, x)} where x ≠ y. Total off-diagonal pairs = n(n - 1) / 2 = 4(3) / 2 = 6 pairs.', 'Step 3: For each diagonal pair (x, x), an anti-symmetric relation can either include it or exclude it. So there are 2 choices per diagonal pair. For 4 pairs: 2⁴ = 16 choices.', 'Step 4: For each pair of off-diagonal elements {(x, y), (y, x)}, an anti-symmetric relation can: (1) Include (x, y) only, (2) Include (y, x) only, or (3) Include neither. It CANNOT include both! So there are 3 choices per pair.', 'Step 5: For 6 off-diagonal pairs: 3⁶ = 729 choices.', 'Step 6: Total anti-symmetric relations = 2⁴ × 3⁶ = 16 × 729 = 11,664.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Counting Anti-Symmetric Relations'}]}, ('EM', 1): {'chapter_number': 2, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Eulerian Circuit vs Eulerian Path', 'warning': 'If a question asks for an Eulerian CIRCUIT, ALL vertices must have even degree! If exactly 2 vertices have odd degree, it has an Eulerian PATH, NOT a circuit.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Core Counting Principles:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Permutation (Order matters):</b> <code>ⁿPᵣ = n! / (n - r)!</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Combination (Order does not matter):</b> <code>ⁿCᵣ = n! / [r! (n - r)!]</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Stars and Bars Theorem (Distributing identical items into distinct bins):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Case 1: Non-negative integer solutions:</b> <code>x₁ + x₂ + ... + xᵣ = n</code> where <code>xᵢ ≥ 0</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Formula:</b> <code>ⁿ⁺ʳ⁻¹Cᵣ₋₁</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Case 2: Positive integer solutions:</b> <code>x₁ + x₂ + ... + xᵣ = n</code> where <code>xᵢ ≥ 1</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Formula:</b> <code>ⁿ⁻¹Cᵣ₋₁</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Derangements (Permutations where NO element appears in its original spot):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Formula: <code>Dₙ = n! × [1 - 1/1! + 1/2! - 1/3! + ... + (-1)ⁿ/n!]</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Useful standard values: D₁ = 0, D₂ = 1, D₃ = 2, D₄ = 9, D₅ = 44.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Pigeonhole Principle (PHP), if n items are put into k containers, at least one container must contain at least ⌈n / k⌉ items!', 'number': '2.1', 'title': 'Permutations, Combinations & Stars-and-Bars'}, {'content': '• <b>The Handshaking Lemma (Fundamental Theorem of Graph Theory):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>∑ deg(v) = 2|E|</code> for all vertices v ∈ V.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Why?</i> Every edge has two endpoints. When you sum all vertex degrees, every edge is counted exactly twice!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Critical Inferences from Handshaking Lemma:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• The sum of degrees in ANY graph is ALWAYS an <b>EVEN</b> integer.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The Odd Degree Theorem:</b> In any undirected graph, the number of vertices with ODD degree is ALWAYS <b>EVEN</b>! You can never have 3 or 5 odd-degree vertices.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Havel-Hakimi Theorem (Testing if a degree sequence is graphic):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Sort the sequence in descending order: d₁, d₂, ..., dₙ.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Remove d₁, and subtract 1 from the next d₁ numbers.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Repeat sorting and reduction. If you reach all zeros, the sequence is graphic! If negative numbers appear, it is NOT graphic.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a k-regular graph with n vertices, total edges |E| = (n × k) / 2.', 'number': '2.2', 'title': 'Handshaking Lemma & Degree Sequences'}, {'content': "• <b>What is a Planar Graph?</b> A graph that can be drawn in a plane such that no two edges cross each other.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Euler's Formula for Connected Planar Graphs:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>V - E + F = 2</code> (where V = vertices, E = edges, F = faces/regions including the unbounded outer face).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Planar Edge Bounds (Essential for GATE MCQs):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• For any simple planar graph with V ≥ 3: <code>E ≤ 3V - 6</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• For a simple planar graph with <b>NO triangles</b> (e.g. bipartite planar graphs): <code>E ≤ 2V - 4</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Kuratowski's Theorem (Planarity Test):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- A graph is planar if and only if it does NOT contain a subgraph that is homeomorphic to (or can be contracted from) <b>K₅</b> (complete graph on 5 vertices) or <b>K₃,₃</b> (complete bipartite utility graph).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: K₅ has V=5, E=10. By 3V-6 = 9, E exceeds bound, so K₅ is non-planar! K₃,₃ has V=6, E=9. By 2V-4 = 8, K₃,₃ is non-planar!', 'number': '2.3', 'title': "Planar Graphs & Euler's Formula"}, {'content': "• <b>Eulerian Graphs (Traverse every EDGE exactly once):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Eulerian Circuit:</b> Connected graph where <b>EVERY vertex has an EVEN degree</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Eulerian Path:</b> Connected graph with <b>EXACTLY TWO vertices of odd degree</b> (path starts at one odd vertex and ends at the other).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Hamiltonian Graphs (Visit every VERTEX exactly once):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• A Hamiltonian cycle visits every vertex and returns to the start.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dirac's Theorem (Sufficient condition):</b> If |V| ≥ 3 and for every vertex deg(v) ≥ |V| / 2, the graph is Hamiltonian.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Graph Coloring & Chromatic Number χ(G):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Chromatic number χ(G) is the minimum colors needed to color vertices so no adjacent vertices share a color.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Bipartite Graph:</b> χ(G) = 2. A graph is 2-colorable (bipartite) iff it contains <b>NO ODD CYCLES</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Four Color Theorem:</b> Every planar graph can be colored with at most 4 colors: χ(G) ≤ 4.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Checking Eulerian graph takes O(V+E) time (just check vertex degrees). Checking Hamiltonian cycle is NP-Complete!', 'number': '2.4', 'title': 'Eulerian & Hamiltonian Graphs, Graph Coloring'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'An Eulerian circuit is a street sweeper who must clean every single street (edge) once. A Hamiltonian cycle is a delivery driver who must visit every home (vertex) once.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Street Sweeper vs Traveling Salesperson'}], 'title': 'Discrete Mathematics - Graph Theory & Combinatorics', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Total faces F = 12 (11 bounded, 1 unbounded).', 'problem': 'A connected planar simple graph has 20 vertices and each vertex has degree 3. How many bounded and unbounded faces does the graph divide the plane into?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Use Handshaking Lemma to find edges E. Sum of degrees = 20 × 3 = 60. So 2E = 60 ⇒ E = 30 edges.', "Step 2: Apply Euler's Formula: V - E + F = 2.", 'Step 3: Substitute V = 20 and E = 30: 20 - 30 + F = 2 ⇒ -10 + F = 2 ⇒ F = 12 total faces.', 'Step 4: Out of 12 total faces, exactly 1 face is the unbounded external face. Therefore, there are 11 bounded faces and 1 unbounded face.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Planar Graph Face Calculation'}]}, ('EM', 2): {'chapter_number': 3, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Eigenvalues vs Eigenvectors Scaling Trap', 'warning': 'Eigenvectors are never unique; any non-zero scalar multiple of an eigenvector is also an eigenvector for the same eigenvalue.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>What is Rank of a Matrix?</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- The maximum number of linearly independent rows (or columns) in the matrix.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- For an m × n matrix A: <code>Rank(A) ≤ min(m, n)</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Properties of Rank (Frequently Tested in GATE):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>Rank(A + B) ≤ Rank(A) + Rank(B)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>Rank(AB) ≤ min(Rank(A), Rank(B))</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>Rank(Aᵀ) = Rank(A)</code> and <code>Rank(Aᵀ A) = Rank(A)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• A square n × n matrix is <b>Invertible (Non-singular)</b> if and only if <code>Rank(A) = n</code> (Full Rank, det(A) ≠ 0).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To find the rank quickly in exam, perform elementary row operations to reduce to Row Echelon Form. The number of non-zero rows is the Rank!', 'number': '3.1', 'title': 'Rank of a Matrix and Echelon Forms'}, {'content': "• <b>Matrix Representation:</b> <code>A X = B</code>, where A is m × n coefficient matrix, X is n × 1 unknown vector, and [A|B] is the augmented matrix.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Master Consistency Decision Matrix:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nCondition                                   │ System Nature │ Solutions\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nRank(A) < Rank([A|B])                       │ Inconsistent  │ NO SOLUTION (0)\nRank(A) = Rank([A|B]) = n (number of vars) │ Consistent    │ UNIQUE SOLUTION (1)\nRank(A) = Rank([A|B]) = r < n               │ Consistent    │ INFINITELY MANY SOLUTIONS\n</div>\n\n• <b>Homogeneous Systems (AX = 0):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Always consistent because X = 0 (Trivial solution) is always a solution!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>det(A) ≠ 0</code> (Rank = n): ONLY the trivial zero solution exists.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>det(A) = 0</code> (Rank < n): Non-trivial (non-zero) infinite solutions exist!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For AX = 0 to have a NON-ZERO solution, det(A) MUST equal 0. This is the single most tested condition in GATE linear algebra!', 'number': '3.2', 'title': 'System of Linear Equations (AX = B)'}, {'content': '• <b>Characteristic Equation:</b> <code>det(A - λI) = 0</code>. Roots of this polynomial are the eigenvalues λ.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>5 Gold-Plated Eigenvalue Theorems:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sum of Eigenvalues = Trace of A</b> (sum of main diagonal elements): <code>∑ λᵢ = Trace(A)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Product of Eigenvalues = Determinant of A</b>: <code>∏ λᵢ = det(A)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If λ is an eigenvalue of A, then:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>kλ</code> is an eigenvalue of <code>kA</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>λᵐ</code> is an eigenvalue of <code>Aᵐ</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>1 / λ</code> is an eigenvalue of <code>A⁻¹</code> (if det(A) ≠ 0).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Eigenvalues of a <b>Triangular</b> or <b>Diagonal</b> matrix are simply its main diagonal entries!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Special Matrices:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Real Symmetric Matrix: All eigenvalues are strictly <b>REAL</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Skew-Symmetric Matrix: All eigenvalues are either <b>pure imaginary</b> or <b>zero</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Orthogonal Matrix: Absolute value of each eigenvalue is <b>1</b> (|λ| = 1).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Idempotent Matrix (A² = A): Eigenvalues are only <b>0 or 1</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Nilpotent Matrix (Aᵏ = 0): ALL eigenvalues are strictly <b>0</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Before doing lengthy characteristic equation algebra, ALWAYS check: (1) Does sum of options equal Trace? (2) Does product of options equal Determinant? 80% of GATE questions are solved in 10 seconds this way!', 'number': '3.3', 'title': 'Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors & Properties'}, {'content': "• <b>Cayley-Hamilton Theorem:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>'Every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation.'</i><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If characteristic equation is <code>λ² - 5λ + 6 = 0</code>, then <code>A² - 5A + 6I = 0</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Multiplying by A⁻¹ gives: <code>A - 5I + 6A⁻¹ = 0 ⇒ A⁻¹ = (5I - A) / 6</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>LU Decomposition (A = L · U):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Factors matrix A into a Lower triangular matrix L and an Upper triangular matrix U.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Doolittle Algorithm:</b> Diagonal elements of L are all 1s (Lᵢᵢ = 1).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Determinant:</b> <code>det(A) = det(L) × det(U) = 1 × ∏ Uᵢᵢ</code> (product of diagonal elements of U).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Solving AX = B: First solve <code>L Y = B</code> (Forward substitution), then solve <code>U X = Y</code> (Back substitution). Takes O(n²) once factorized!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: LU decomposition exists for matrix A without row exchanges if and only if all leading principal submatrices of A have non-zero determinants.', 'number': '3.4', 'title': 'Cayley-Hamilton Theorem & LU Decomposition'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "Google's original PageRank algorithm models the entire internet as a giant transition matrix. The rank of every website on Earth is literally the principal eigenvector of that matrix!", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Google PageRank & Eigenvectors'}], 'title': 'Linear Algebra - Systems, Eigenvalues & LU Decomposition', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Eigenvalues are 1 and 6.', 'problem': 'Find the eigenvalues of matrix A = [[2, -1], [-4, 5]].', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate Trace(A) = sum of diagonal elements = 2 + 5 = 7.', 'Step 2: Calculate Determinant det(A) = (2)(5) - (-1)(-4) = 10 - 4 = 6.', 'Step 3: We need two eigenvalues λ₁, λ₂ such that λ₁ + λ₂ = 7 and λ₁ · λ₂ = 6.', 'Step 4: Solve the quadratic equation λ² - (Trace)λ + det(A) = 0 ⇒ λ² - 7λ + 6 = 0.', 'Step 5: Factorize: (λ - 1)(λ - 6) = 0 ⇒ λ₁ = 1, λ₂ = 6.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Finding Eigenvalues using Trace and Det'}]}, ('EM', 3): {'chapter_number': 4, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Independent vs Mutually Exclusive Trap', 'warning': 'Mutually exclusive means P(A ∩ B) = 0 (cannot happen together). Independent means P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B). If P(A) > 0 and P(B) > 0, events CANNOT be both mutually exclusive and independent!'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>L'Hôpital's Rule for Indeterminate Limits:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Applicable ONLY for forms <code>0 / 0</code> or <code>∞ / ∞</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>lim [f(x) / g(x)] = lim [f'(x) / g'(x)]</code> (Differentiate numerator and denominator separately until determinate).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Maxima and Minima Checklist:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Find critical points by setting first derivative to zero: <code>f'(x) = 0</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Compute second derivative <code>f''(x)</code> at critical point c:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>f''(c) < 0</code>: Local <b>MAXIMUM</b> at c.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>f''(c) > 0</code>: Local <b>MINIMUM</b> at c.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>f''(c) = 0</code>: Test fails (use higher derivatives or first derivative sign test).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Differentiability ALWAYS implies Continuity. But Continuity DOES NOT imply Differentiability (e.g. f(x) = |x| is continuous at 0 but not differentiable at 0).', 'number': '4.1', 'title': 'Calculus: Limits, Continuity & Maxima-Minima'}, {'content': "• <b>Conditional Probability:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B)</code> (where P(B) > 0).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Independent Events:</b> A and B are independent iff <code>P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B)</code>. (Notice: Mutually exclusive events with non-zero probability can NEVER be independent!).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Bayes' Theorem Formula:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>P(Bᵢ | A) = [P(A | Bᵢ) · P(Bᵢ)] / [∑ P(A | Bⱼ) · P(Bⱼ)]</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <i>Intuition:</i> Updates the prior probability P(Bᵢ) after observing new evidence A to get the posterior probability P(Bᵢ | A).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Bayes medical testing questions, even with a 99% accurate test, if the disease is extremely rare (e.g. 0.1%), the probability of actually having the disease given a positive test is surprisingly low due to false positives!', 'number': '4.2', 'title': "Probability Axioms & Bayes' Theorem"}, {'content': "• <b>Discrete Distributions:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nDistribution │ PMF P(X = k)                  │ Mean E[X] │ Variance Var(X)\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nBinomial     │ ⁿCₖ pᵏ (1-p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ               │ np        │ np(1 - p)\nPoisson      │ (e⁻ᵝ · λᵏ) / k!               │ λ         │ λ (Mean = Variance!)\nGeometric    │ (1-p)ᵏ⁻¹ · p                  │ 1 / p     │ (1 - p) / p²\n</div>\n\n• <b>Continuous Distributions:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Uniform Distribution on [a, b]:</b> Mean = (a + b) / 2, Variance = (b - a)² / 12.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Exponential Distribution (Parameter λ):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- PDF: <code>f(x) = λ e⁻ᵝˣ</code> (for x ≥ 0). Mean = 1/λ, Variance = 1/λ².<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Memoryless Property:</b> <code>P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t)</code>. The past waiting time gives zero information about future waiting time!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Normal (Gaussian) Distribution:</b> Bell curve, Symmetric about mean μ. 68.2% data within μ ± σ, 95.4% within μ ± 2σ, 99.7% within μ ± 3σ.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For Poisson distribution, Mean is ALWAYS equal to Variance! If an exam question gives Mean = 4, Variance is guaranteed to be 4.', 'number': '4.3', 'title': 'Discrete & Continuous Probability Distributions'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'An exponential distribution represents electronic components. If a lightbulb has survived 100 hours, the probability it lasts another 10 hours is identical to a brand new bulb lasting 10 hours.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Memoryless Lightbulb'}], 'title': 'Calculus & Probability - Distributions and Bayes Theorem', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Probability is approximately 4.72%.', 'problem': 'A rare disease affects 0.1% of a population. A diagnostic test is 99% accurate for sick people, but has a 2% false positive rate for healthy people. If a randomly chosen person tests positive, what is the probability they actually have the disease?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Define events: D = Person has disease, T = Test is positive. P(D) = 0.001, P(~D) = 0.999.', 'Step 2: Test sensitivities: P(T | D) = 0.99, P(T | ~D) = 0.02.', 'Step 3: Calculate Total Probability P(T) = P(T|D)P(D) + P(T|~D)P(~D) = (0.99)(0.001) + (0.02)(0.999) = 0.00099 + 0.01998 = 0.02097.', "Step 4: Apply Bayes' Rule: P(D | T) = P(T|D)P(D) / P(T) = 0.00099 / 0.02097 ≈ 0.0472 (4.72%)."], 'title': "Worked Example: Bayes' Theorem Medical Diagnostic Test"}]}, ('ALGO', 0): {'chapter_number': 16, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Master Theorem Non-Polynomial Difference Trap', 'warning': "For T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n log n: here a=2, b=2, k=1, p=1. Many think it's Case 3 because of log n, but a = bᵏ is Case 2, giving Θ(n log² n)."}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>The 5 Formal Asymptotic Notations:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Big-O (O):</b> Asymptotic Upper Bound: <code>f(n) ≤ c · g(n)</code> for all n ≥ n₀.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Big-Omega (Ω):</b> Asymptotic Lower Bound: <code>f(n) ≥ c · g(n)</code> for all n ≥ n₀.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Big-Theta (Θ):</b> Tight Bound: <code>c₁ · g(n) ≤ f(n) ≤ c₂ · g(n)</code> (Both O and Ω hold).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Little-o (o):</b> Strict upper bound: <code>lim [f(n) / g(n)] = 0</code> as n → ∞.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Little-omega (ω):</b> Strict lower bound: <code>lim [f(n) / g(n)] = ∞</code> as n → ∞.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Master Growth Rate Hierarchy (Slowest to Fastest):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>O(1) < O(log log n) < O(log n) < O(√n) < O(n) < O(n log n) < O(n²) < O(n³) < O(2ⁿ) < O(n!) < O(nⁿ)</code><br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Limit Comparison Test:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;To compare f(n) and g(n), evaluate <code>L = lim [f(n) / g(n)]</code> as n → ∞:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If L = 0: f(n) = o(g(n)).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If 0 < L < ∞: f(n) = Θ(g(n)).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If L = ∞: f(n) = ω(g(n)).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: n^(1/log n) = 2^(log n / log n) = 2¹ = Constant O(1)! (log n)! is polynomial (Θ(n^(log log n))), whereas 2^(log n) = n.', 'number': '16.1', 'title': 'Asymptotic Growth Hierarchy & Big-O Definitions'}, {'content': "• <b>Standard Form:</b> <code>T(n) = a T(n / b) + f(n)</code> (where a ≥ 1, b > 1, f(n) = Θ(nᵏ logᵖ n)).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Comparison: Compare a with bᵏ:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nCondition               │ Recurrence Result T(n)\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\na > bᵏ (log_b(a) > k)   │ T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))\na = bᵏ (log_b(a) = k)   │ \n  - If p > -1           │ T(n) = Θ(nᵏ log^(p+1) n)\n  - If p = -1           │ T(n) = Θ(nᵏ log(log n))\n  - If p < -1           │ T(n) = Θ(nᵏ)\na < bᵏ (log_b(a) < k)   │\n  - If p ≥ 0            │ T(n) = Θ(nᵏ logᵖ n)\n  - If p < 0            │ T(n) = Θ(nᵏ)\n</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Master theorem FAILS if: (1) a is not constant (e.g. 2ⁿ T(n/2)), (2) b < 1, or (3) f(n) is not polynomially larger/smaller (e.g. T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n/log n).', 'number': '16.2', 'title': 'Master Theorem for Divide & Conquer Recurrences'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'A quadtree map splits into 4 sub-tiles each time you zoom in. Master theorem calculates the exact rendering time required to draw millions of map tiles at any zoom level.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Zoomable Digital Map'}], 'title': 'Asymptotic Analysis & Recurrence Relations', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'T(n) = Θ(n² log² n)', 'problem': 'Solve the recurrence relation: T(n) = 4 T(n / 2) + n² log n.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify parameters: a = 4, b = 2, f(n) = n² log n.', 'Step 2: Compare a with bᵏ: here k = 2, p = 1. So bᵏ = 2² = 4.', 'Step 3: Since a = bᵏ (4 = 4), we are in Case 2.', 'Step 4: Check power p of log: p = 1 > -1.', 'Step 5: Apply formula: T(n) = Θ(nᵏ log^(p+1) n) = Θ(n² log² n).'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Solving Recurrence using Master Theorem'}]}, ('ALGO', 1): {'chapter_number': 17, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'The Selection Sort Stability Trap', 'warning': 'Selection sort is NOT stable because swapping elements over long distances can flip the relative order of identical elements (e.g. sorting [4a, 4b, 1] swaps 4a with 1, placing 4a after 4b).'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Comprehensive Comparison Table:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nAlgorithm      │ Best Time     │ Average Time  │ Worst Time    │ Aux Space │ Stable? │ In-Place?\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nBubble Sort    │ O(n)          │ O(n²)         │ O(n²)         │ O(1)      │ YES     │ YES\nInsertion Sort │ O(n)          │ O(n²)         │ O(n²)         │ O(1)      │ YES     │ YES\nSelection Sort │ O(n²)         │ O(n²)         │ O(n²)         │ O(1)      │ NO      │ YES\nMerge Sort     │ O(n log n)    │ O(n log n)    │ O(n log n)    │ O(n)      │ YES     │ NO\nQuick Sort     │ O(n log n)    │ O(n log n)    │ O(n²)         │ O(log n)  │ NO      │ YES\nHeap Sort      │ O(n log n)    │ O(n log n)    │ O(n log n)    │ O(1)      │ NO      │ YES\nCounting Sort  │ O(n + k)      │ O(n + k)      │ O(n + k)      │ O(n + k)  │ YES     │ NO\nRadix Sort     │ O(d(n + k))   │ O(d(n + k))   │ O(d(n + k))   │ O(n + k)  │ YES     │ NO\n</div>\n\n• <b>Comparison-Based Sorting Lower Bound:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Any comparison-based sorting algorithm requires at least <b>Ω(n log n) comparisons</b> in the worst case (proven via Decision Tree model of height ⌈log₂(n!)⌉).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Insertion Sort is optimal for nearly sorted arrays (runs in O(n) time). Quick Sort worst case occurs when pivot is always extreme (already sorted array with first/last element as pivot).', 'number': '17.1', 'title': 'Master Summary Table of Sorting Algorithms'}, {'content': "• <b>Collision Resolution Techniques:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Separate Chaining (Open Hashing):</b> Linked list at each hash bucket. Load factor <code>α = n / m</code> can exceed 1. Search time = <code>O(1 + α)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Open Addressing (Closed Hashing):</b> All elements stored in the hash table itself (α ≤ 1):<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Linear Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + i) mod m</code>. Suffers from <b>Primary Clustering</b> (long runs of occupied slots).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Quadratic Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + c₁i + c₂i²) mod m</code>. Eliminates primary clustering, but suffers from <b>Secondary Clustering</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Double Hashing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h₁(k) + i · h₂(k)) mod m</code>. Eliminates all clustering! <code>h₂(k)</code> must be relatively prime to m.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Double Hashing, h₂(k) must NEVER evaluate to 0! If h₂(k) = 0, the probe sequence remains trapped in an infinite loop at the initial slot.', 'number': '17.2', 'title': 'Hashing, Collision Resolution & Open Addressing'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'A hash table is a coat check room where your receipt number directly points to your coat hanger slot. Separate chaining hangs multiple coats on the same hook.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Coat Rack in a Restaurant'}], 'title': 'Sorting, Searching & Hashing', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Final Table: [Slot 0: 42, Slot 3: 101, Slot 4: 25, Slot 5: 96, Slot 6: 102]', 'problem': 'Insert keys [25, 42, 96, 101, 102] into a hash table of size 7 using hash function h(k) = k mod 7 and Linear Probing.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Table size m = 7 (slots 0 to 6).', 'Step 2: Key 25: 25 mod 7 = 4. Slot 4 is empty ⇒ Insert 25 at Slot 4.', 'Step 3: Key 42: 42 mod 7 = 0. Slot 0 is empty ⇒ Insert 42 at Slot 0.', 'Step 4: Key 96: 96 mod 7 = 5. Slot 5 is empty ⇒ Insert 96 at Slot 5.', 'Step 5: Key 101: 101 mod 7 = 3. Slot 3 is empty ⇒ Insert 101 at Slot 3.', 'Step 6: Key 102: 102 mod 7 = 4. Slot 4 is occupied (collision!). Probe slot (4+1)%7 = 5 (occupied!). Probe slot (4+2)%7 = 6 (empty!) ⇒ Insert 102 at Slot 6.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Linear Probing Hash Table Insertion'}]}, ('ALGO', 2): {'chapter_number': 18, 'exam_traps': [{'title': '0/1 Knapsack Greedy Trap', 'warning': 'Never use the Value/Weight ratio for 0/1 Knapsack! Greedy only guarantees optimal solution for Fractional Knapsack, not 0/1 Knapsack.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Greedy Choice Property:</b> Makes the locally optimal choice at each step with the hope that this leads to a globally optimal solution.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Activity Selection Problem:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Maximize number of non-overlapping activities.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Greedy Strategy:</b> Sort activities in ascending order of their <b>FINISH TIMES</b>! Always pick the next compatible activity that finishes earliest. Runs in <code>O(n log n)</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Huffman Coding (Optimal Prefix Codes):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Place all characters in a Min-Priority Queue keyed by frequency.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Repeatedly extract two smallest nodes, merge them into a new internal node with frequency = sum, and reinsert.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Cost of tree: <code>L_avg = ∑ (frequencyᵢ × depthᵢ)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Generates a <b>Full Binary Tree</b>: exactly <code>2n - 1</code> total nodes for n characters!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Huffman Coding, if all character frequencies are Fibonacci numbers (1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8...), the generated tree is completely skewed, yielding maximum variance in codeword lengths!', 'number': '18.1', 'title': 'Greedy Method: Activity Selection & Huffman Coding'}, {'content': '• <b>When to use DP?</b> Problems with (1) <b>Overlapping Subproblems</b> and (2) <b>Optimal Substructure</b>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Fractional Knapsack (Greedy) vs 0/1 Knapsack (DP):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Fractional Knapsack:</b> Items can be broken. Greedy by <code>Value / Weight</code> ratio works! O(n log n).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>0/1 Knapsack:</b> Items cannot be split. Greedy fails! Solved via DP in <code>O(n · W)</code> time (Pseudo-polynomial; NP-Complete).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Longest Common Subsequence (LCS):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Given strings X of length m and Y of length n:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <code>X[i] == Y[j]</code>: <code>LCS[i, j] = 1 + LCS[i-1, j-1]</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <code>X[i] != Y[j]</code>: <code>LCS[i, j] = max(LCS[i-1, j], LCS[i, j-1])</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Table size: (m + 1) × (n + 1). Time and space: <code>O(m · n)</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Matrix Chain Multiplication: Multiplying matrices A₁ (d₀×d₁) ... Aₙ (dₙ₋₁×dₙ) has recurrence m[i, j] = min_{k} { m[i,k] + m[k+1,j] + d_{i-1} d_k d_j } where i ≤ k < j. Number of ways to parenthesize n matrices = Catalan number C_{n-1}.', 'number': '18.2', 'title': 'Dynamic Programming: 0/1 Knapsack & LCS'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Dynamic programming avoids re-calculating routes. If the optimal path from Delhi to Mumbai passes through Jaipur, the optimal route from Jaipur to Mumbai is already stored and reused.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: GPS Route Planning'}], 'title': 'Design Techniques - Greedy & Dynamic Programming', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Average codeword length is 1.9 bits.', 'problem': 'Given characters A, B, C, D with frequencies 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1, find the average codeword length of the optimal Huffman code.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Merge lowest two frequencies: C (0.2) + D (0.1) = Node CD (0.3).', 'Step 2: Remaining: A (0.4), B (0.3), CD (0.3). Merge lowest two: B (0.3) + CD (0.3) = Node BCD (0.6).', 'Step 3: Merge remaining two: A (0.4) + BCD (0.6) = Root (1.0).', 'Step 4: Compute codeword lengths (depth in tree):', '  - A is at depth 1: length = 1 bit.', '  - B is at depth 2: length = 2 bits.', '  - C and D are at depth 3: length = 3 bits each.', 'Step 5: Average length L = (0.4 × 1) + (0.3 × 2) + (0.2 × 3) + (0.1 × 3) = 0.4 + 0.6 + 0.6 + 0.3 = 1.9 bits/char.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Huffman Code Length Calculation'}]}, ('ALGO', 3): {'chapter_number': 19, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Adding Constant to Edge Weights Trap', 'warning': 'Adding a positive constant c to ALL edge weights changes the shortest path (paths with more edges are penalized more!), but DOES NOT change the Minimum Spanning Tree!'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Traversals Comparison:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>BFS (Breadth-First Search):</b> Uses a <b>Queue</b>. Finds the <b>shortest path in unweighted graphs</b>! Edge types: Tree edges and Cross edges.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DFS (Depth-First Search):</b> Uses a <b>Stack</b> (or recursion). Edge types: Tree, Back, Forward, Cross edges.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Both run in <code>O(V + E)</code> time using adjacency list.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Cycle Detection Rules:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Undirected Graph:</b> Cycle exists iff BFS/DFS encounters a visited node that is NOT the parent.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Directed Graph:</b> Cycle exists iff DFS discovers a <b>BACK EDGE</b> (pointing to an active ancestor on the recursion stack)!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Topological Sort (for Directed Acyclic Graphs - DAGs):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Linear ordering where for every directed edge u → v, vertex u appears before v.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Possible if and only if the graph has <b>NO CYCLES</b> (is a DAG).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Kahn's Algorithm: Repeatedly remove vertices with in-degree 0.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A directed graph has a unique topological sort if and only if there exists a Hamiltonian path in the DAG.', 'number': '19.1', 'title': 'BFS vs DFS Traversals & Edge Classifications'}, {'content': "• <b>What is a Spanning Tree?</b> A subgraph that connects all V vertices with exactly <code>V - 1</code> edges and no cycles.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Kruskal's vs Prim's Algorithms:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nAlgorithm │ Data Structure Used                │ Time Complexity │ Better Suited For\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nKruskal   │ Disjoint Set Union (DSU / Find-Union)│ O(E log E)      │ Sparse Graphs (E ≈ V)\nPrim      │ Min-Heap (Priority Queue)          │ O(E log V)      │ Dense Graphs (E ≈ V²)\n</div>\n\n• <b>Cut Property & Uniqueness of MST:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Cut Property:</b> For any cut in the graph, the minimum weight crossing edge MUST be in the MST.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Uniqueness:</b> If all edge weights in a connected graph are <b>DISTINCT</b>, the graph has a <b>UNIQUE Minimum Spanning Tree</b>!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If all edge weights are distinct, MST is strictly unique! If edge weights are not distinct, multiple MSTs may exist, but all MSTs have the EXACT SAME total weight.', 'number': '19.2', 'title': 'Minimum Spanning Tree (Kruskal & Prim)'}, {'content': "• <b>Master Shortest Path Decision Matrix:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nAlgorithm       │ Strategy │ Works with Negative Weights? │ Detects Negative Cycles? │ Time Complexity\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nDijkstra        │ Greedy   │ NO (Fails with negative wts) │ NO                       │ O((V + E) log V)\nBellman-Ford    │ DP       │ YES                          │ YES                      │ O(V · E)\nFloyd-Warshall  │ DP (All) │ YES (No negative cycles)     │ YES                      │ O(V³)\n</div>", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Dijkstra's algorithm FAILS with negative weight edges because it greedily assumes that adding more edges to an already finalized vertex can never decrease its distance!", 'number': '19.3', 'title': 'Shortest Paths (Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford, Floyd-Warshall)'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'An electrical power grid uses an MST to connect every town to the power station with minimum total copper wire, ensuring zero redundant circular loops.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Power Grid & Fiber Optic Cabling'}], 'title': 'Graph Algorithms - Traversals, MST & Shortest Paths', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Total MST weight is 11.', 'problem': "Given a graph with vertices {A, B, C, D, E} and edges with weights: (A-B: 1), (B-C: 2), (A-C: 2), (C-D: 3), (B-D: 4), (D-E: 5), (C-E: 6). Find the total weight of the MST using Kruskal's algorithm.", 'steps': ['Step 1: Total vertices V = 5. An MST must contain exactly V - 1 = 4 edges.', 'Step 2: Sort edges by weight: (A-B: 1), (B-C: 2), (A-C: 2), (C-D: 3), (B-D: 4), (D-E: 5), (C-E: 6).', 'Step 3: Select (A-B: 1) ⇒ No cycle. Edge count = 1.', 'Step 4: Select (B-C: 2) ⇒ No cycle. Edge count = 2.', 'Step 5: Inspect (A-C: 2) ⇒ A, B, C are already connected! Adding A-C creates cycle A-B-C-A. REJECT A-C.', 'Step 6: Select (C-D: 3) ⇒ Connects D to {A, B, C}. No cycle. Edge count = 3.', 'Step 7: Inspect (B-D: 4) ⇒ Creates cycle B-C-D-B. REJECT B-D.', 'Step 8: Select (D-E: 5) ⇒ Connects E. No cycle. Edge count = 4. Stop!', 'Step 9: Total MST weight = 1 + 2 + 3 + 5 = 11.'], 'title': "Worked Example: Kruskal's MST Edge Selection"}]}, ('COA', 0): {'chapter_number': 9, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'The PC-Relative Pre-Increment Trap', 'warning': 'Students frequently forget to update PC before calculating target address. In a 2-byte instruction at address 1000 with offset +20, PC becomes 1002, so Target = 1002 + 20 = 1022, NOT 1020!'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>What is the Machine Instruction Cycle?</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;The sequence of operations the CPU performs to execute a single machine instruction:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Fetch:</b> Program Counter (PC) places address on Memory Address Register (MAR). Instruction read into Memory Data Register (MDR) then to Instruction Register (IR). PC increments to point to next instruction.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Decode:</b> Control Unit decodes the opcode and determines addressing modes and required operands.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Execute:</b> ALU carries out the operation (e.g., addition, logical AND, branch condition evaluation).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Store (Write-back):</b> Result written to destination register or memory location.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Instruction Formats by Number of Addresses:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>3-Address Format:</b> <code>ADD R1, R2, R3</code> (R1 ← R2 + R3). Expressive, but needs large instruction word length.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>2-Address Format:</b> <code>ADD R1, R2</code> (R1 ← R1 + R2). One operand acts as both source and destination.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>1-Address Format:</b> <code>ADD X</code> (AC ← AC + M[X]). Uses an implicit <b>Accumulator (AC)</b> register.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>0-Address Format:</b> <code>ADD</code> (TOS ← TOS + SOS). Uses an implicit evaluation <b>Stack</b> (Push/Pop operations).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Expanding Opcode Technique:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Allows instructions with different numbers of addresses to coexist within a fixed-length instruction word.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If an n-bit opcode field has unused patterns, those unused bit combinations are extended into adjacent operand address fields to encode instructions with fewer operands!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In expanding opcode calculation: Unused opcodes at level k = (Total possible opcodes) - (Used opcodes). Each unused opcode expands into 2^m opcodes at the next level!', 'number': '9.1', 'title': 'Instruction Cycle, Formats & Expanding Opcodes'}, {'content': "• <b>What is Effective Address (EA)?</b> The physical or logical address of the actual data operand in main memory.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Comprehensive Master Addressing Modes Guide:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nAddressing Mode    │ Effective Address (EA) Formula │ Memory References to Fetch Operand │ Primary Exam Use Case\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nImmediate          │ Operand is inside instruction  │ 0 (Fastest! No memory lookup)      │ Loading constants (e.g. R1 ← 5)\nDirect (Absolute)  │ EA = Address field (A)         │ 1 memory read                      │ Global static variables\nIndirect           │ EA = M[A] (Pointer to pointer) │ 2 memory reads (Dereferencing)     │ Passing pointers / parameters\nRegister           │ Operand is inside register R   │ 0 memory reads (Register file)     │ High-speed local variables\nRegister Indirect  │ EA = [R]                       │ 1 memory read                      │ Array / pointer traversals\nPC-Relative        │ EA = PC + Offset (Displacement)│ 1 memory read (if in memory)       │ Relocatable branch jumps (if/else)\nIndexed            │ EA = Base (A) + Index Reg (R)  │ 1 memory read                      │ Array subscript access A[i]\nBase Register      │ EA = Base Reg (R) + Offset (A) │ 1 memory read                      │ Program relocation in memory\nAuto-Increment     │ EA = [R], then R ← R + d       │ 1 memory read                      │ Step through array elements\n</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In PC-Relative addressing, when calculating EA = PC + Offset, remember that the PC has ALREADY BEEN INCREMENTED to point to the NEXT instruction! Never use the address of the current instruction.', 'number': '9.2', 'title': 'Addressing Modes & Effective Address (EA) Mechanics'}, {'content': '• <b>Hardwired Control Unit:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Implemented as fixed combinational logic circuits (Decoders, Multiplexers, Logic Gates).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Pros:</b> Extremely fast; minimal delay.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Cons:</b> Rigid and inflexible; changing an instruction requires redesigning silicon chip layout.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Used in:</b> RISC architectures.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Microprogrammed Control Unit:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Control signals generated by reading micro-instructions stored in a special high-speed memory called <b>Control ROM (Control Store)</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Pros:</b> Highly flexible; adding new instructions is just writing new micro-code in ROM.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Cons:</b> Slower than hardwired due to ROM memory access time.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Used in:</b> CISC architectures.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Horizontal vs Vertical Micro-instructions:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Horizontal (Unencoded):</b> 1 bit per control signal. Fast, allows parallel control signals, but very wide word size (wastes memory).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Vertical (Encoded):</b> Control signals grouped into n fields. An m-bit field decodes into 2ᵐ signals via decoders. Narrow word size, but slightly slower due to decoder delay.', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In vertical microprogramming, if k mutually exclusive control signals need to be generated, the minimum control field size is ⌈log₂(k + 1)⌉ bits (the +1 is for the 'no operation' state).", 'number': '9.3', 'title': 'Control Unit Design: Hardwired vs Microprogrammed'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Immediate mode is cash handed directly to you. Direct mode is going to a street address written on a card. Indirect mode is finding a locker key at that address with instructions leading to another locker.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Mailing Addresses & Addressing Modes'}], 'title': 'Machine Instructions & Addressing Modes', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': '(a) Immediate: 0 operand reads, value=400; (b) Direct: 1 operand read, value=500; (c) Indirect: 2 operand reads, value=600.', 'problem': 'An instruction is stored at memory location 300 with its address field holding value 400. The value stored at location 400 is 500, and value at location 500 is 600. How many memory accesses are required and what operand value is fetched if the addressing mode is: (a) Immediate, (b) Direct, (c) Indirect?', 'steps': ['Step 1: In all cases, 1 memory fetch is initially needed to read the instruction itself from location 300.', 'Step 2: (a) Immediate Mode: The operand is 400 directly from the instruction register. Additional memory accesses = 0. Operand = 400.', 'Step 3: (b) Direct Mode: Effective address EA = 400. Fetch M[400] = 500. Additional memory accesses = 1. Operand = 500.', 'Step 4: (c) Indirect Mode: Read location 400 to get address 500 (1st memory access), then read location 500 to fetch operand 600 (2nd memory access). Total additional memory accesses = 2. Operand = 600.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Memory References in Addressing Modes'}]}, ('COA', 1): {'chapter_number': 10, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Implicit Q₋₁ = 0 Omission Trap', 'warning': "When counting Booth's operations, students often forget that Q₋₁ is initialized to 0! If the LSB of Q is 1, the first pair is (1, 0) which triggers a subtraction."}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Why Booth's Algorithm?</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Multiplies two signed 2's complement numbers without requiring sign-magnitude conversion!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Replaces long strings of consecutive 1s with a single addition and subtraction (e.g. 7 in binary is 0111 = 8 - 1 = 2³ - 2⁰).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Step-by-Step Booth's Execution Rules:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Initialize: Accumulator AC = 0, Multiplier in Q, Q₋₁ = 0, Count = n.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;At each step, inspect the pair (Q₀, Q₋₁):<br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>\n(Q₀, Q₋₁) Pair │ Action Performed\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n0  0           │ No arithmetic operation (Just Shift)\n0  1           │ AC ← AC + M (End of a string of 1s)\n1  0           │ AC ← AC - M (Beginning of a string of 1s)\n1  1           │ No arithmetic operation (Middle of a string of 1s)\n</div>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;After arithmetic operation: <b>Arithmetic Right Shift [AC, Q, Q₋₁]</b> by 1 bit (preserving MSB of AC). Decrement count until 0.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Performance Analysis (GATE Favorite):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Best Case (Fewest Operations):</b> Multiplier with alternating blocks of all 0s or all 1s (e.g. 00001111).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Worst Case (Maximum Operations):</b> Multiplier with alternating 0s and 1s (e.g. 01010101), where every cycle requires an add or subtract!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Booth's algorithm, the number of arithmetic additions and subtractions equals the number of transitions (0→1 and 1→0) between adjacent bits of the multiplier (including implicit Q₋₁ = 0)!", 'number': '10.1', 'title': "Booth's Multiplication Algorithm for Signed 2's Complement"}, {'content': '• <b>Restoring Division:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Left shift [A, Q]. Subtract Divisor M: <code>A ← A - M</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If result A is negative: Set Q₀ = 0 and <b>Restore A</b>: <code>A ← A + M</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If result A is positive: Set Q₀ = 1 (no restoration needed).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Non-Restoring Division (Faster Hardware):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Avoids restoring addition when sign is negative!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If previous A was positive: Shift left, then <b>Subtract M</b> (A ← 2A - M).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If previous A was negative: Shift left, then <b>Add M</b> (A ← 2A + M).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Saves clock cycles because every step does exactly 1 shift and 1 arithmetic operation.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Non-restoring division takes exactly n cycles for n-bit numbers, whereas restoring division may take up to 2n operations in worst-case.', 'number': '10.2', 'title': 'Restoring vs Non-Restoring Integer Division'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "Booth's multiplication is like paying $99: instead of handing over 99 individual $1 bills, you hand over a $100 bill and take back $1 in change (100 - 1).", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Cashier Change Shortcut'}], 'title': 'ALU, Control Unit & Memory Interfacing', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Exactly 2 arithmetic operations (1 addition, 1 subtraction).', 'problem': "How many additions and subtractions are performed when multiplying by 00111100 using Booth's algorithm?", 'steps': ['Step 1: Write down the multiplier with Q₋₁ = 0 appended at the end: Q = 0 0 1 1 1 1 0 0, Q₋₁ = 0.', 'Step 2: Inspect adjacent bit pairs from right to left (Qᵢ, Qᵢ₋₁):', '  - Pair 1 (Bit 0, Q₋₁): (0, 0) ⇒ No operation.', '  - Pair 2 (Bit 1, Bit 0): (0, 0) ⇒ No operation.', '  - Pair 3 (Bit 2, Bit 1): (1, 0) ⇒ SUBTRACTION.', '  - Pair 4 (Bit 3, Bit 2): (1, 1) ⇒ No operation.', '  - Pair 5 (Bit 4, Bit 3): (1, 1) ⇒ No operation.', '  - Pair 6 (Bit 5, Bit 4): (1, 1) ⇒ No operation.', '  - Pair 7 (Bit 6, Bit 5): (0, 1) ⇒ ADDITION.', '  - Pair 8 (Bit 7, Bit 6): (0, 0) ⇒ No operation.', 'Step 3: Total operations = 1 Addition + 1 Subtraction = 2 arithmetic operations.'], 'title': "Worked Example: Counting Operations in Booth's Algorithm"}]}, ('COA', 2): {'chapter_number': 11, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Byte-Addressable vs Word-Addressable Trap', 'warning': 'If memory is 4-byte Word-Addressable, the block offset identifies the WORD within the block, NOT the byte! Students lose marks by calculating byte offsets for word-addressed machines.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Main Memory Address Breakdown Formulas:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Given: Main Memory Size = 2ᴹ bytes (M-bit address), Cache Size = C bytes, Block/Line Size = B = 2ᵇ bytes.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Block Offset bits (w):</b> <code>w = log₂(Block Size B)</code> (Identifies the exact byte inside the cache block).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>The Three Cache Mapping Schemes:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nMapping Scheme          │ Address Field Partitioning                   │ Cache Index Formula\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nDirect Mapping          │ [ Tag (M - c) ] [ Line Index (c - w) ] [ Word (w) ] │ Line = (Block Address) mod (Total Lines)\nFully Associative       │ [ Tag (M - w) ] [ Word Offset (w) ]          │ Block can go in ANY line (No index bits!)\nK-Way Set Associative   │ [ Tag (M - s - w) ] [ Set Index (s) ] [ Word (w) ]   │ Set = (Block Address) mod (Number of Sets)\n</div>\n\n• <b>K-Way Set Associative Details:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Number of Sets:</b> <code>S = (Total Cache Lines) / K = Cache Size / (K × Block Size)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Set Index bits (s):</b> <code>s = log₂(S)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Tag bits:</b> <code>Tag = M - s - w</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Comparators Required:</b> Exactly <b>K comparators</b> operating in parallel, each comparing Tag bits with the stored tag in that set.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Total Cache Directory / Tag Memory Size = (Number of Lines) × (Tag bits + Valid bit + Dirty bit). GATE questions often ask for Tag overhead in bits or KB!', 'number': '11.1', 'title': 'Cache Address Partitioning (Direct, Associative, Set-Associative)'}, {'content': '• <b>Cache Write Policies:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Through:</b> Updates both Cache and Main Memory simultaneously on every write. Always consistent, but slower writes (solved with Write Buffers).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Back:</b> Updates ONLY the Cache block. Sets a <b>Dirty Bit = 1</b>. Main memory is updated only when the block is evicted! Much faster writes.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Write-Allocate vs No-Write-Allocate (on Write Miss):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Write-Allocate: Fetch missed block into cache then write (typically paired with Write-Back).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- No-Write-Allocate: Write directly to main memory without loading into cache (paired with Write-Through).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Replacement Algorithms (used on cache miss when set is full):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LRU (Least Recently Used):</b> Evicts block untouched for the longest time. Hardware tracking: K-way set associative requires ⌈log₂(K!)⌉ or K(K-1)/2 bits per set.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>FIFO (First-In, First-Out):</b> Evicts oldest loaded block (ignores recency of access).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Random:</b> Evicts a pseudo-random block (very cheap hardware, surprisingly good performance).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fully Associative cache has 0 conflict misses (only cold and capacity misses). Direct-mapped cache suffers heavily from conflict misses!', 'number': '11.2', 'title': 'Cache Write Policies & Replacement Algorithms'}, {'content': '• <b>Single-Level AMAT Formula:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>AMAT = H₁ × T₁ + (1 - H₁) × Tₘₑₘ</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Alternative (Add Hit Time to Miss Penalty): <code>AMAT = T₁ + (1 - H₁) × Miss_Penalty</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Two-Level Cache Hierarchy (L1 and L2):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>AMAT = T₁ + (1 - H₁) × [T₂ + (1 - H₂_local) × Tₘₑₘ]</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Local Hit Rate (H_local):</b> Hits in L2 divided by total accesses that reached L2.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Global Hit Rate (H_global):</b> Hits in L2 divided by total CPU memory requests.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Relationship: <code>H₂_global = (1 - H₁) × H₂_local</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In simultaneous access memory, all levels are searched together: AMAT = H₁T₁ + (H₂ - H₁)T₂ + ... In hierarchical access, L2 is searched ONLY AFTER L1 misses!', 'number': '11.3', 'title': 'Average Memory Access Time (AMAT) & Multi-Level Caches'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'CPU registers are the pens in your hand. L1 cache is the textbook open on your desk. L2 is your backpack. Main memory is the bookshelf across the room. Disk is the city public library!', 'title': "Real-Life Analogy: The Student's Study Desk"}], 'title': 'Memory Hierarchy, Cache Mapping & AMAT', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Tag = 18 bits, Set Index = 10 bits, Word Offset = 4 bits.', 'problem': 'A computer system has 32-bit byte-addressable main memory. The cache is 64 KB, 4-way set associative with 16-byte cache blocks. Determine the size of the Tag, Set Index, and Word Offset fields.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Total address bits M = 32 bits.', 'Step 2: Block size B = 16 bytes = 2⁴ bytes ⇒ Word Offset bits w = 4 bits.', 'Step 3: Total cache lines = Cache Size / Block Size = 64 KB / 16 bytes = 65,536 / 16 = 4096 lines.', 'Step 4: Number of Sets S = Total lines / K = 4096 / 4 = 1024 sets = 2¹⁰ sets ⇒ Set Index bits s = 10 bits.', 'Step 5: Tag bits = Total bits - Set bits - Word bits = 32 - 10 - 4 = 18 bits.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating Tag, Set and Word Bits'}]}, ('COA', 3): {'chapter_number': 12, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Branch Resolved in ID vs EX Stage Trap', 'warning': 'Read carefully whether the branch condition and target address are resolved in the ID stage (causes 1 stall) or the EX stage (causes 2 stalls).'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>The 5 Pipeline Stages:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>IF (Instruction Fetch):</b> Read instruction from I-cache using PC. Increment PC.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>ID (Instruction Decode / Register Fetch):</b> Decode opcode and read source registers.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>EX (Execution / Effective Address):</b> ALU executes arithmetic/logic operation or computes EA for load/store.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>MEM (Memory Access):</b> Read from or write to D-cache for Load/Store instructions. Other instructions are idle.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>WB (Write Back):</b> Write result from ALU or memory into destination register.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Cycle Time & Speedup Formula:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Clock Cycle Time (τ):</b> <code>τ = max(Stage Delays) + Register Delay</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Time to execute n instructions on a k-stage pipeline: <code>T_pipe = [k + (n - 1)] × τ</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Ideal Speedup over non-pipelined execution: <code>Speedup S = (n × k) / (k + n - 1)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• For large n (as n → ∞): <b>Maximum Theoretical Speedup = k</b> (the number of stages)!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The pipeline clock frequency is strictly determined by the SLOWEST stage! Speeding up non-bottleneck stages gives zero improvement to the clock cycle.', 'number': '12.1', 'title': 'The 5-Stage Classic RISC Pipeline & Clock Cycle'}, {'content': '• <b>1. Structural Hazards (Hardware Resource Conflicts):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Occurs when two instructions compete for the same hardware unit in the same clock cycle.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Classic example:</i> Memory conflict if instruction fetch (IF) and data access (MEM) share a single unified memory.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solution:</i> Harvard architecture (Separate I-Cache and D-Cache).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>2. Data Hazards (Data Dependencies):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RAW (Read After Write - True Dependency):</b> Instruction J tries to read register before Instruction I writes it! Most common hazard in GATE.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>WAR (Write After Read - Anti-Dependency):</b> Instruction J tries to write before Instruction I reads.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>WAW (Write After Write - Output Dependency):</b> Instruction J writes before Instruction I writes.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <i>Solutions for RAW:</i><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Operand Forwarding (Bypassing):</b> Route ALU output directly from EX/MEM buffer to EX stage input of next cycle (eliminates stalls for ALU-to-ALU).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Load-Use Delay:</b> A Load instruction followed immediately by an instruction that uses its result ALWAYS requires <b>1 stall cycle</b> even with forwarding!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>3. Control Hazards (Branch Penalty):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Target PC is not known until branch condition is evaluated in ID or EX stage.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>Solutions:</i> Branch prediction (Static: predict taken/not taken; Dynamic: 2-bit saturating counter), Branch delay slots.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: With full operand forwarding: ALU-to-ALU instructions cause 0 stalls! Load-to-ALU instructions cause exactly 1 stall cycle.', 'number': '12.2', 'title': 'Pipeline Hazards: Structural, Data & Control'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Pipelining is like doing laundry: while load 2 is in the washer, load 1 is in the dryer, and load 0 is being folded. You finish a load every 30 minutes instead of waiting 90 minutes per load!', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Industrial Laundromat Pipeline'}], 'title': 'Instruction Pipelining, Hazards & Performance', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Total execution time is 20.8 ns.', 'problem': 'A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 150 ps, 120 ps, 180 ps, 160 ps, and 100 ps. The pipeline register delay is 20 ps. How long does it take to execute 100 independent instructions?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Find the bottleneck stage delay = max(150, 120, 180, 160, 100) = 180 ps.', 'Step 2: Calculate clock cycle time τ = max stage delay + register delay = 180 ps + 20 ps = 200 ps.', 'Step 3: Number of stages k = 5, number of instructions n = 100.', 'Step 4: Total clock cycles required = k + n - 1 = 5 + 100 - 1 = 104 cycles.', 'Step 5: Total execution time = 104 × 200 ps = 20,800 ps = 20.8 ns.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Pipeline Execution Time and Stalls'}]}, ('PDS', 0): {'chapter_number': 13, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'The Sequence Point & Undefined Behavior Trap', 'warning': "Expressions like 'i = i++ + ++i' modify variable i multiple times without an intervening sequence point. In C standard, this is strictly UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR! GATE questions frequently ask if such code is valid or undefined."}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>1. What is a Pointer? (Basic Definition & Memory Model):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- A normal variable stores a data value: <code>int x = 25;</code> (stores 25 at some address, say 1000).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- A pointer variable stores the <b>memory address</b> of another variable: <code>int *p = &x;</code> (stores address 1000).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- The dereference operator (<code>*p</code>) reads or writes the value located at that stored address (<code>*p = 50</code> changes x to 50).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>2. What is an Array in Memory?</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Suppose you declare: <code>int arr[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};</code> (assuming 4 bytes per integer):<br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-emerald-400 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed'>\nArray Element:   arr[0]      arr[1]      arr[2]      arr[3]\nStored Value:      10          20          30          40\nMemory Address:  [1000]      [1004]      [1008]      [1012]\n                    ↑\n                   arr (points here)\n</div>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The array name <code>arr</code> is a CONSTANT POINTER:</b> It holds the base address: <code>arr == &arr[0] == 1000</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Because it is constant, you <b>CANNOT reassign it</b>: <code>arr++</code> or <code>arr = p</code> causes a compilation error!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>3. Why does <code>3[arr] == arr[3]</code> work? (The Subscript Rule):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• The C compiler does NOT know what square brackets <code>[]</code> mean in assembly language!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Every time you write <code>arr[i]</code>, the compiler literally rewrites it as:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>arr[i] ≡ *(arr + i)</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Since addition is commutative (<code>a + b == b + a</code>), <code>*(arr + i)</code> is identical to <code>*(i + arr)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• But <code>*(i + arr)</code> can be written back into bracket notation as <code>i[arr]</code>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Therefore:</b> <code>arr[i] == *(arr + i) == *(i + arr) == i[arr]</code>! In C, writing <code>3[arr]</code> evaluates to 40!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>4. Pointer Arithmetic & Scaling Rule:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Adding 1 to a pointer does NOT add 1 byte! It adds <code>1 × sizeof(*p)</code> bytes.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>p = 1000</code> (pointer to 4-byte int): <code>p + 1 = 1004</code>, <code>p + 2 = 1008</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>char *cp = 1000</code>: <code>cp + 1 = 1001</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>double *dp = 1000</code>: <code>dp + 1 = 1008</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>5. Pointer Increment Nuances (*p++ vs (*p)++ vs *++p):</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nExpression   │ Precedence & Step-by-Step Execution                             │ Value Returned │ Effect on Pointer / Memory\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n*p++         │ Postfix ++ has higher precedence than *. Reads *p first;        │ Original *p    │ Pointer p moves to next slot!\n             │ then increments pointer p.                                      │                │\n(*p)++       │ Parentheses force *p first. Fetches current value; then         │ Original *p    │ Value in memory incremented!\n             │ increments the INTEGER in memory by 1.                          │                │ Pointer p DOES NOT MOVE!\n*++p         │ Prefix ++ increments pointer p to next slot first;              │ Value at new   │ Pointer p moves to next slot!\n             │ then dereferences that new address.                             │ address        │\n++*p         │ Dereferences *p to get value; then increments that integer      │ Incremented    │ Value in memory incremented!\n             │ before using it.                                                │ new value      │ Pointer p DOES NOT MOVE!\n</div>\n\n• <b>6. The Giant Trap: <code>arr + 1</code> vs <code>&arr + 1</code>:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>arr</code> has type <code>int*</code>: Pointer to first element. <code>arr + 1</code> jumps <b>4 bytes</b> to <code>&arr[1]</code> (1004).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>&arr</code> has type <code>int (*)[4]</code>: Pointer to the ENTIRE array of 4 integers!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Therefore, <code>&arr + 1</code> jumps the <b>ENTIRE ARRAY of 4 ints (16 bytes)</b> to 1016!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When an array is passed into a function (like void solve(int arr[])), the array automatically DECAYS into a simple pointer (int *arr)! Inside that function, sizeof(arr) returns the size of a POINTER (8 bytes on 64-bit systems), NOT the size of the array!', 'number': '13.1', 'title': 'Pointers & Array Equivalence in C (Complete Conceptual Breakdown)'}, {'content': '• <b>Operator Precedence Hierarchy in C (Top to Bottom):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Postfix operators: <code>() [] -> . ++ --</code> (Left-to-Right)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Unary prefix operators: <code>! ~ ++ -- + - * & (type) sizeof</code> (Right-to-Left)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Multiplicative: <code>* / %</code> (Left-to-Right)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Additive: <code>+ -</code> (Left-to-Right)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;5. Relational: <code>< <= > >=</code> (Left-to-Right)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;6. Equality: <code>== !=</code> (Left-to-Right)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;7. Logical AND: <code>&&</code> (Left-to-Right - Short-circuit)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;8. Logical OR: <code>||</code> (Left-to-Right - Short-circuit)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;9. Conditional ternary: <code>? :</code> (Right-to-Left)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;10. Assignment: <code>= += -= *= ...</code> (Right-to-Left)<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;11. Comma operator: <code>,</code> (Left-to-Right - Lowest precedence)<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Short-Circuit Evaluation Mechanics:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>In Logical AND (expr1 && expr2):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 == 0 (False)</code>, the whole result MUST be False.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 IS NEVER EVALUATED!</b> Any side effects (like <code>i++</code>) inside expr2 are completely skipped!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>In Logical OR (expr1 || expr2):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>expr1 != 0 (True)</code>, the whole result MUST be True.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>expr2 IS NEVER EVALUATED!</b> Any side effects inside expr2 are completely skipped!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In expression: int a=0, b=5; if (a++ && ++b) ..., a++ evaluates to 0 (post-increment), so && short-circuits! ++b is NEVER executed. At the end, a = 1, b = 5.', 'number': '13.2', 'title': 'Short-Circuit Evaluation & Operator Precedence'}, {'content': '• <b>The Anatomy of a Recursive Call Stack Frame:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Every function call pushes an <b>Activation Record (Stack Frame)</b> containing:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Function parameters.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Return address (where to jump back when function finishes).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Local variables.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Saved frame pointer.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Tracing Recursive Functions (Pre-order vs Post-order Actions):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Code written <b>BEFORE</b> the recursive call executes on the way DOWN the recursion tree (Pre-order).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Code written <b>AFTER</b> the recursive call executes on the way UP during backtracking (Post-order / unwinding).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Static Variables inside Recursive Functions:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>static int count = 0;</code> is initialized ONLY ONCE when program loads into memory (in the Data Segment).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• It does NOT reside on the stack! Every recursive call shares the <b>exact same copy</b> of the static variable!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In recursive questions with static or global variables: modifications persist across calls! Never reset static variables when unwinding the call stack.', 'number': '13.3', 'title': 'Recursion, Call Stacks & Static Storage'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "A variable is the contents of Hotel Room 104. A pointer is the room key card displaying 'Room 104'. Dereferencing (*p) is using the key card to open the door and access what is inside.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Hotel Room Keys & Pointers'}], 'title': 'Programming in C & Recursion Tracing', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Output is: 10 20 30 21', 'problem': 'What is the exact output of the following C program?\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\\n    int *p = a;\\n    int val1 = *p++;\\n    int val2 = (*p)++;\\n    int val3 = *++p;\\n    printf("%d %d %d %d", val1, val2, val3, a[1]);\\n    return 0;\\n}', 'steps': ['Step 1: Initially array a = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}. Pointer p points to a[0] (value 10, address 1000).', "Step 2: Line 'int val1 = *p++;': Postfix ++ increments pointer p AFTER dereference. val1 receives *p = 10. Pointer p moves to point to a[1] (address 1004).", "Step 3: Line 'int val2 = (*p)++;': Parentheses force *p first. val2 receives current value of a[1] = 20. Then the value in memory at a[1] is incremented from 20 to 21! Pointer p remains at a[1].", "Step 4: Line 'int val3 = *++p;': Prefix ++ increments pointer p first. p now points to a[2] (address 1008). Then dereferences: val3 = a[2] = 30.", 'Step 5: Inspect printed values: val1 = 10, val2 = 20, val3 = 30, a[1] = 21.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Comprehensive Pointer Code Trace'}]}, ('PDS', 1): {'chapter_number': 14, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Stack Element Count from Infix Conversions', 'warning': 'GATE often asks for the MAXIMUM number of operators stored simultaneously on the stack during infix conversion. Be sure to count parentheses if they are pushed onto the operator stack.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Stack (LIFO - Last In, First Out):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;All insertions and deletions happen at one end called the <b>Top</b>. Time complexity for Push and Pop is O(1).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Infix to Postfix Conversion Rules (Shunting-Yard Algorithm):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Scan infix expression from left to right.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If operand (number/variable): Output it directly.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If '(': Push to stack.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. If ')': Pop and output operators from stack until matching '(' is found. Discard both parentheses.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;5. If operator op: While stack is non-empty and <code>Precedence(Stack Top) ≥ Precedence(op)</code>, pop and output. Then push op onto stack.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Exception:</i> For Right-to-Left associative operators (like exponentiation <code>^</code>), pop only if strictly greater.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Evaluating Postfix Expression using Stack:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Scan postfix from left to right.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If operand: Push onto stack.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If operator: Pop two operands: <code>op2 = pop()</code>, <code>op1 = pop()</code>. Compute <code>result = op1 (operator) op2</code>. Push result back onto stack!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In postfix evaluation: First popped is the RIGHT operand (op2), second popped is the LEFT operand (op1)! For non-commutative operations like division (op1 / op2) and subtraction (op1 - op2), order is critical.', 'number': '14.1', 'title': 'Stack Infix, Postfix & Evaluation Algorithms'}, {'content': "• <b>Circular Queue Modulo Arithmetic:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Solves the 'false overflow' problem of linear array queues.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Enqueue:</b> <code>rear = (rear + 1) % Capacity; queue[rear] = item;</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dequeue:</b> <code>item = queue[front]; front = (front + 1) % Capacity;</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Queue Full Condition:</b> <code>(rear + 1) % Capacity == front</code> (one slot left empty to distinguish full from empty).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Queue Empty Condition:</b> <code>front == rear</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Implementing a Queue using Two Stacks (Stack1 and Stack2):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Enqueue(x):</b> Push x onto Stack1. (O(1)).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dequeue():</b> If Stack2 is empty, pop ALL elements from Stack1 and push them into Stack2 (reverses order!). Then pop from Stack2. (Amortized O(1) time).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An array of size N can hold at most N - 1 elements in a standard circular queue if front == rear indicates empty.', 'number': '14.2', 'title': 'Circular Queues & Two-Stack Queue Implementation'}, {'content': "• <b>Singly vs Doubly Linked List Time Complexities:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Insertion at head: O(1).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Deletion of given node: O(1) in Doubly Linked List; O(n) in Singly (need predecessor pointer).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Floyd's Tortoise and Hare Cycle Detection:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Use two pointers: <code>slow</code> (advances 1 step) and <code>fast</code> (advances 2 steps).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Loop Detection:</b> If <code>slow == fast</code> at any point, a cycle exists!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Finding Start Node of Cycle:</b> When slow and fast meet, reset <code>slow = head</code> and keep <code>fast</code> at meeting point. Move both at 1 step per cycle. <b>They will meet exactly at the cycle start node!</b>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Reversing a singly linked list in-place takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space using 3 pointers: prev, curr, next.', 'number': '14.3', 'title': "Linked Lists & Floyd's Cycle Finding Algorithm"}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "Every webpage URL you visit is pushed onto a navigation stack. Clicking 'Back' pops the current page and reveals the previous URL.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Browser Back Button'}], 'title': 'Linear Data Structures - Stacks, Queues & Linked Lists', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Postfix expression: A B C D ^ E - * +', 'problem': 'Convert the infix expression: A + B * (C ^ D - E) into postfix notation.', 'steps': ["Step 1: Scan 'A' ⇒ Output: A. Stack: [].", "Step 2: Scan '+' ⇒ Stack: [+].", "Step 3: Scan 'B' ⇒ Output: A B. Stack: [+].", "Step 4: Scan '*' ⇒ Precedence(*) > Precedence(+), so push. Stack: [+, *].", "Step 5: Scan '(' ⇒ Push to stack. Stack: [+, *, (].", "Step 6: Scan 'C' ⇒ Output: A B C. Stack: [+, *, (].", "Step 7: Scan '^' ⇒ Push. Stack: [+, *, (, ^].", "Step 8: Scan 'D' ⇒ Output: A B C D. Stack: [+, *, (, ^].", "Step 9: Scan '-' ⇒ Precedence(-) < Precedence(^), so pop '^'. Output: A B C D ^. Push '-'. Stack: [+, *, (, -].", "Step 10: Scan 'E' ⇒ Output: A B C D ^ E. Stack: [+, *, (, -].", "Step 11: Scan ')' ⇒ Pop until '(': pops '-'. Output: A B C D ^ E -. Discard '('. Stack: [+, *].", "Step 12: End of input reached. Pop remaining operators: '*' then '+'. Output: A B C D ^ E - * +."], 'title': 'Worked Example: Infix to Postfix Conversion'}]}, ('PDS', 2): {'chapter_number': 15, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Heap Search Complexity Trap', 'warning': 'Searching for an arbitrary key in a binary heap takes O(n) time, NOT O(log n)! A heap is not a BST; there is no relationship between left and right siblings.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Fundamental Binary Tree Properties:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. In any binary tree, the number of leaf nodes L is related to the number of nodes of degree 2 (n₂) by:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b><code>L = n₂ + 1</code></b> (Leaf nodes = Internal nodes with 2 children + 1).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Maximum nodes in a binary tree of height h: <code>N_max = 2^(h + 1) - 1</code> (taking root height = 0).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Minimum height of a binary tree with N nodes: <code>h_min = ⌈log₂(N + 1)⌉ - 1</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Tree Traversals:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Preorder:</b> Root → Left → Right.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Inorder:</b> Left → Root → Right.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Postorder:</b> Left → Right → Root.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Level-Order:</b> Breadth-first using a queue.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Tree Reconstruction:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- To construct a <b>unique</b> binary tree, you MUST have the <b>INORDER traversal</b> along with either PREORDER or POSTORDER traversal!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Given only Preorder and Postorder, a unique tree CANNOT be constructed unless every internal node has exactly 2 children (Strict binary tree).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The Inorder traversal of a Binary Search Tree (BST) ALWAYS yields elements in strictly increasing sorted order! This is the #1 property used in GATE tree questions.', 'number': '15.1', 'title': 'Binary Tree Mathematical Properties & Traversals'}, {'content': '• <b>BST Property:</b> For every node X: all keys in Left Subtree < X.key < all keys in Right Subtree.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Operations & Time Complexity:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Search, Insertion, Deletion: Average case = <code>O(log n)</code>; Worst case (skewed tree) = <code>O(n)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Deletion Cases:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Node is a leaf: Simply remove it.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Node has 1 child: Replace node with its child.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Node has 2 children: Replace with its <b>Inorder Predecessor</b> (max in left subtree) OR <b>Inorder Successor</b> (min in right subtree).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Counting Structurally Unique BSTs (Catalan Numbers):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Number of structurally distinct BSTs possible with n unlabeled nodes equals the n-th Catalan number:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Cₙ = (1 / (n + 1)) × ²ⁿCₙ</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Standard values: C₁ = 1, C₂ = 2, C₃ = 5, C₄ = 14, C₅ = 42.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;If n keys are distinct and labeled, total trees = <code>n! × Cₙ</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To find Inorder Successor of node X: If X has a right child, it is the leftmost node in right subtree. If X has NO right child, it is the lowest ancestor whose left subtree contains X.', 'number': '15.2', 'title': 'Binary Search Trees (BST) & Catalan Numbers'}, {'content': '• <b>What is a Binary Heap?</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;A <b>Complete Binary Tree (CBT)</b> stored compactly in an array that satisfies the heap property:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Max-Heap:</b> <code>Parent ≥ Children</code> (Root is maximum).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Min-Heap:</b> <code>Parent ≤ Children</code> (Root is minimum).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Array Index Mapping (1-Based Indexing):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parent of node i: <code>⌊i / 2⌋</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Left Child of node i: <code>2i</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Right Child of node i: <code>2i + 1</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Leaves reside from index <code>⌊n / 2⌋ + 1</code> to <code>n</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Time Complexities of Heap Operations:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Insertion: <code>O(log n)</code> (bubbling up).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Extract-Max / Extract-Min: <code>O(log n)</code> (heapify down).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>BUILD-HEAP (from arbitrary array):</b> Takes <b>O(n) time</b> (NOT O(n log n)!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>Why?</i> Most nodes are near the leaves where height is small: ∑ (h / 2ʰ) converges to a constant!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Build-Heap takes strictly O(n) linear time. Heap-Sort takes O(n log n) time because Extract-Max is called n times.', 'number': '15.3', 'title': 'Binary Heaps & Bottom-Up O(n) Build-Heap'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'A priority queue (heap) manages emergency patients. The patient with the highest medical severity is always at the root and treated first, regardless of arrival order.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Hospital Emergency Room Triage'}], 'title': 'Trees, BST & Binary Heaps', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Postorder: D, E, B, F, C, A', 'problem': 'Given Preorder: [A, B, D, E, C, F] and Inorder: [D, B, E, A, F, C], find the Postorder traversal.', 'steps': ['Step 1: First element of Preorder is root ⇒ Root = A.', 'Step 2: Find A in Inorder: [D, B, E] are in Left Subtree; [F, C] are in Right Subtree.', 'Step 3: In Preorder, next elements for Left Subtree are [B, D, E] with root = B.', 'Step 4: In Inorder [D, B, E], D is left child of B, E is right child of B.', 'Step 5: For Right Subtree [F, C], Preorder has [C, F] with root = C. In Inorder [F, C], F is left child of C.', 'Step 6: Compute Postorder (Left, Right, Root): D, E, B, F, C, A.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Reconstructing Tree from Inorder and Preorder'}]}, ('CD', 0): {'chapter_number': 23, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Left Recursion Elimination Formula Trap', 'warning': "To eliminate A → Aα | β: rewrite as A → β A' and A' → α A' | ε. Notice β comes FIRST! If you write A → α A', the language is ruined."}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Lexical Analysis (Scanner):</b> Reads stream of characters and groups them into <b>Tokens</b> using Regular Expressions and DFA. Strips comments and whitespace.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Rules for Calculating FIRST(X):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. If X is a terminal: <code>FIRST(X) = {X}</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If X → ε is a production: add ε to FIRST(X).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If X → Y₁ Y₂ ... Yₖ: add FIRST(Y₁) \\ {ε} to FIRST(X). If Y₁ derives ε, add FIRST(Y₂) \\ {ε}, and so forth until some Yᵢ does not derive ε. If all derive ε, add ε.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Rules for Calculating FOLLOW(A) (Applies ONLY to Non-Terminals!):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Add <code>$</code> (end of input marker) to <code>FOLLOW(S)</code> (Start Symbol).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If production <code>A → α B β</code>: everything in <code>FIRST(β) \\ {ε}</code> is in <code>FOLLOW(B)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If production <code>A → α B</code> OR <code>A → α B β</code> where <code>β ⇒* ε</code>: everything in <code>FOLLOW(A)</code> is in <code>FOLLOW(B)</code>!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: FOLLOW set can NEVER contain the empty string ε! If ε appears in your FOLLOW set calculation, you made an error.', 'number': '23.1', 'title': 'Lexical Tokens & Calculating FIRST and FOLLOW Sets'}, {'content': '• <b>What is LL(1)?</b> Left-to-right scan, Leftmost derivation, 1 lookahead token.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Conditions for a Grammar to be LL(1):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;For every production with multiple choices <code>A → α | β</code>:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <code>FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅</code> (No common prefix).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. At most one of α or β can derive ε.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If <code>β ⇒* ε</code>, then <code>FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Instant LL(1) Disqualifiers (Must-Know for GATE):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Left Recursive Grammar:</b> <code>A → Aα</code> can NEVER be LL(1)! Must eliminate left recursion first.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Ambiguous Grammar:</b> Can NEVER be LL(1)!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Non-Deterministic (Common Prefix):</b> <code>A → aB | aC</code> can NEVER be LL(1)! Must left-factor first.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In the LL(1) parse table, if any cell [A, a] contains MULTIPLE production entries, the grammar is NOT LL(1)!', 'number': '23.2', 'title': 'LL(1) Parser Conditions & Grammatical Disqualifiers'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "An LL(1) parser is like reading JSON: looking at the single next character ('{' or '[') immediately tells the parser whether to parse an Object or an Array.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: JSON Parser Lookahead'}], 'title': 'Lexical Analysis & Top-Down Parsing (LL(1))', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'FIRST(S) = {a, b, c}, FOLLOW(A) = {b, c}.', 'problem': 'Given grammar: S → ABc, A → a | ε, B → b | ε. Compute FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(A).', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate FIRST(A) = {a, ε}. Calculate FIRST(B) = {b, ε}.', "Step 2: For S → ABc: FIRST(S) includes FIRST(A)\\{ε} = {a}. Since A can be ε, include FIRST(B)\\{ε} = {b}. Since B can also be ε, include terminal 'c'. So FIRST(S) = {a, b, c}.", 'Step 3: For FOLLOW(A): Look where A appears on RHS: S → ABc. Here A is followed by Bc. So FOLLOW(A) includes FIRST(Bc)\\{ε} = FIRST(B)\\{ε} ∪ {c} = {b, c}.', "Step 4: Check if Bc can derive ε: Bc has terminal 'c', so it cannot derive ε. Hence FOLLOW(S) is not added."], 'title': 'Worked Example: Computing FIRST and FOLLOW Sets'}]}, ('CD', 1): {'chapter_number': 24, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'LALR State Count Assumption Trap', 'warning': 'LALR(1) table size is ALWAYS identical to LR(0) and SLR(1) tables! It never has the larger state count of CLR(1).'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Bottom-Up Parsing Mechanics:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Constructs parse tree from the leaves (tokens) up to the root (Start symbol).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Traces a <b>Rightmost Derivation in REVERSE</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Actions: <b>Shift</b> (push token to stack), <b>Reduce</b> (replace handle with LHS non-terminal), <b>Accept</b>, <b>Error</b>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>The 4 LR Parsers Compared:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nParser   │ Item Type Used │ Reduce Action Placed In                  │ States Count Comparison\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nLR(0)    │ [A → α · β]    │ In ALL input cells of that state row     │ Smallest (Base DFA)\nSLR(1)   │ [A → α · β]    │ ONLY in FOLLOW(A) cells of that row      │ Identical to LR(0) states!\nLALR(1)  │ [A → α · β, a] │ ONLY in lookahead 'a' cells              │ Identical to LR(0) states! (Merged)\nCLR(1)   │ [A → α · β, a] │ ONLY in lookahead 'a' cells              │ Largest number of states!\n</div>\n\n• <b>Parser Power Hierarchy (Strict Inclusions):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ CLR(1)</code> and <code>LL(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ CLR(1)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• CLR(1) is the most powerful deterministic parser, but requires massive state tables.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• LALR(1) merges states of CLR(1) that have identical LR(0) cores, preserving table size while keeping high parsing power!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Merging CLR(1) states to form LALR(1) can NEVER create a Shift-Reduce (S-R) conflict! However, it CAN potentially introduce Reduce-Reduce (R-R) conflicts.', 'number': '24.1', 'title': 'Shift-Reduce Parsing & The 4 LR Parsers'}, {'content': '• <b>Shift-Reduce (S-R) Conflict:</b> Occurs when the parser cannot decide whether to shift the next input symbol or reduce the current handle: state has both <code>[A → α · a β]</code> and <code>[B → γ ·]</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Reduce-Reduce (R-R) Conflict:</b> Occurs when two distinct productions are ready to be reduced simultaneously in the same state: state has both <code>[A → α ·]</code> and <code>[B → β ·]</code>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An ambiguous grammar can NEVER be parsed by ANY LR parser (LR(0), SLR(1), LALR(1), CLR(1)) because ambiguity inherently creates unresolvable conflicts!', 'number': '24.2', 'title': 'Shift-Reduce and Reduce-Reduce Conflicts'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Unix tools like Yacc and GNU Bison are LALR(1) parser generators because LALR(1) gives the maximum grammar coverage with the minimum table memory.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Yacc and Bison Tooling'}], 'title': 'Bottom-Up Parsing (LR Parsers) & Parser Comparisons', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Exactly 15 states.', 'problem': 'If the canonical collection of LR(0) items for a grammar has 15 states, what is the number of states in the SLR(1) parsing table?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Recall the state count definition for SLR(1).', 'Step 2: SLR(1) uses the EXACT same canonical collection of LR(0) items as LR(0). It only changes where reduction actions are placed in the table (placing them in FOLLOW(A) instead of all columns).', 'Step 3: Therefore, the number of states in SLR(1) is ALWAYS exactly equal to the number of states in LR(0).', 'Step 4: States in SLR(1) = 15.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: State Count in SLR(1) vs LALR(1) vs CLR(1)'}]}, ('CD', 2): {'chapter_number': 25, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Three-Address Code Temporary Variable Minimization Trap', 'warning': 'When asked for the MINIMUM number of temporary variables to represent an expression in 3-address code, reuse temporary variables whose values are no longer live!'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Synthesized vs Inherited Attributes:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Synthesized Attribute:</b> Computed from children nodes in parse tree (Bottom-up flow).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Inherited Attribute:</b> Computed from parent or sibling nodes (Top-down / Left-to-Right flow).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>S-Attributed Definition:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Uses <b>ONLY Synthesized Attributes</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Can be naturally evaluated during bottom-up parsing (LR parsers) using stack values!<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>L-Attributed Definition:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Uses Synthesized attributes AND restricted Inherited attributes.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>The L-Restriction Rule:</b> In production <code>A → X₁ X₂ ... Xₙ</code>, an inherited attribute of Xᵢ can depend ONLY on:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Attributes of symbols to its <b>LEFT</b> (X₁, X₂, ..., Xᵢ₋₁).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Inherited</b> attributes of parent A.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- It can NEVER depend on symbols to its right or synthesized attributes of parent A!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every S-Attributed SDD is an L-Attributed SDD (S-Attributed ⊂ L-Attributed). S-attributed SDDs are evaluated during bottom-up LR parsing; L-attributed SDDs are evaluated during depth-first left-to-right traversals.', 'number': '25.1', 'title': 'S-Attributed vs L-Attributed Syntax-Directed Translations'}, {'content': "• <b>Machine-Independent Optimizations:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Folding:</b> Compute compile-time constants: <code>x = 3 + 4</code> becomes <code>x = 7</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Propagation:</b> If <code>x = 7</code>, replace subsequent <code>y = x * 2</code> with <code>y = 14</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Common Subexpression Elimination:</b> Avoid recomputing identical expressions.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dead Code Elimination:</b> Remove instructions whose results are never read.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Loop Invariant Code Motion (Hoisting):</b> Move computations that don't change inside the loop outside before the loop begins.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strength Reduction:</b> Replace expensive operations with cheaper ones (e.g. replace <code>x * 2</code> with <code>x + x</code> or <code>x << 1</code>).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Data Flow Analysis Framework:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Liveness Analysis:</b> A variable is <i>live</i> at point p if its current value may be used along some path starting from p. Computed <b>Backwards</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Reaching Definitions:</b> Which definitions d may reach point p. Computed <b>Forwards</b>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Loop Invariant code motion and strength reduction yield the largest asymptotic runtime speedups in compiled binary loops.', 'number': '25.2', 'title': 'Code Optimization & Data Flow Analysis'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'The gcc compiler flag -O3 runs all loop optimizations, constant propagation, and dead code elimination to produce blazing fast machine code without altering program behavior.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: GCC -O3 Optimization Flag'}], 'title': 'SDT, Intermediate Code & Optimization (Data Flow Analysis)', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'NOT L-Attributed (depends on attribute to the right).', 'problem': 'Consider production A → B C with semantic rule: B.in = C.syn + 2. Is this SDD L-Attributed?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Check attribute types: B.in is an inherited attribute of non-terminal B.', 'Step 2: Check dependency: B.in depends on C.syn.', 'Step 3: Inspect position in production: Symbol C is to the RIGHT of B in production A → B C.', 'Step 4: Recall the L-Attributed definition: An inherited attribute of a symbol can depend ONLY on symbols to its LEFT or inherited attributes of parent.', 'Step 5: Since B depends on an attribute to its right (C), it violates the L-attributed condition!'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Identifying L-Attributed SDD Violations'}]}, ('OS', 0): {'chapter_number': 26, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Counting Semaphore Sign Value Trap', 'warning': 'When S is negative (e.g. S = -4), there are 4 processes in the blocked queue! Do not confuse negative semaphore values with errors.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>How fork() Works:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Creates a brand new child process that is an EXACT replica of the parent process (copies memory address space, registers, file descriptors).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Return Value of fork():</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>0</b> to the CHILD process.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>Child's PID (> 0)</b> to the PARENT process.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>-1</b> on failure.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Number of Processes Created by n Consecutive fork() Calls:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Total processes = <b>2ⁿ</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Number of newly created child processes = <b>2ⁿ - 1</b>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Process Tree Execution Trace with Loops:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;In <code>for(int i=0; i<3; i++) fork();</code>:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- i=0: 1 process becomes 2.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- i=1: 2 processes become 4.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- i=2: 4 processes become 8. Total = 2³ = 8 processes!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In fork() code with print statements: Unbuffered printf (printf('X\\n')) flushes immediately. Buffered printf without newline (printf('X')) copies the buffered character into the child, causing doubled prints!", 'number': '26.1', 'title': 'The fork() System Call & Process Trees'}, {'content': "• <b>The Critical Section Problem:</b> Ensuring multiple concurrent processes access shared resources without race conditions.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>The 3 Mandatory Criteria for Critical Section:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion (Mandatory):</b> If process Pi is executing in its critical section, no other process can enter.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Progress (Mandatory):</b> If no process is in critical section, only processes wishing to enter can compete, and decision cannot be postponed indefinitely (NO deadlock!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Bounded Waiting (Optional for basic correctness, mandatory for fairness):</b> There must be a bound on the number of times other processes can enter before a requesting process is granted entry (NO starvation!).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Peterson's Algorithm for 2 Processes:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Uses shared <code>flag[2]</code> (interest) and <code>turn</code> (polite tie-breaker):<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>flag[i] = true; turn = j; while (flag[j] && turn == j); // Critical Section flag[i] = false;</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Satisfies Mutual Exclusion, Progress, and Bounded Waiting!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strict alternation (using only 'turn' variable) satisfies Mutual Exclusion, but FAILS Progress because a process cannot re-enter if the other process is not interested!", 'number': '26.2', 'title': "Critical Section Criteria & Peterson's Solution"}, {'content': '• <b>Semaphore Definition:</b> An integer synchronization variable accessed ONLY via two atomic operations:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Wait(S) / P(S):</b> <code>S--; if (S < 0) block();</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Signal(S) / V(S):</b> <code>S++; if (S <= 0) wakeup();</code><br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Counting Semaphore Value Interpretation:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <code>S > 0</code>: Value represents the number of available resources.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <code>S = 0</code>: All resources in use, no processes waiting.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <code>S < 0</code>: Magnitude <code>|S|</code> represents the <b>exact number of processes currently blocked in the waiting queue</b>!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a counting semaphore is initialized to 10, and 6 Wait operations and 2 Signal operations are performed: Current value = 10 - 6 + 2 = 6.', 'number': '26.3', 'title': 'Counting & Binary Semaphores'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'A binary semaphore is a single restroom key hanging on the restaurant wall. If the hook is empty, you must wait until the occupant returns the key.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Restaurant Restroom Key'}], 'title': 'Processes, Threads, Concurrency & Synchronization', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Total 6 processes created.', 'problem': 'How many total processes are created by the following C code?\\n\\nint main() {\\n    fork();\\n    if (fork() == 0) {\\n        fork();\\n    }\\n    return 0;\\n}', 'steps': ['Step 1: Start with 1 original parent process P₀.', 'Step 2: First fork(): P₀ creates child C₁. Now 2 processes exist: {P₀, C₁}.', 'Step 3: Second fork() called by both P₀ and C₁: P₀ creates C₂, and C₁ creates C₃. Now 4 processes exist: {P₀, C₁, C₂, C₃}.', 'Step 4: Check if condition (fork() == 0): This evaluates to True ONLY in the newly created child processes (C₂ and C₃).', 'Step 5: Third fork() is executed ONLY by C₂ and C₃: C₂ creates child C₄, and C₃ creates child C₅.', 'Step 6: Total processes = 4 + 2 = 6 processes.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Counting fork() Child Processes'}]}, ('OS', 1): {'chapter_number': 27, 'exam_traps': [{'title': "Banker's Algorithm Resource Request Check Trap", 'warning': 'When evaluating a Resource Request, you MUST check BOTH: (1) Request ≤ Need, AND (2) Request ≤ Available! If either fails, the process must wait immediately.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Scheduling Metrics:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Turnaround Time (TAT):</b> <code>Completion Time - Arrival Time</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Waiting Time (WT):</b> <code>Turnaround Time - Burst Time</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Response Time (RT):</b> Time from arrival to first execution on CPU.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Master Comparison of Scheduling Algorithms:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nAlgorithm                      │ Preemptive? │ Optimal For                  │ Major Weakness / Pitfall\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nFCFS (First-Come, First-Served)│ NO          │ Simple batch systems         │ Convoy Effect (Long process blocks short)\nSJF (Shortest Job First)       │ NO          │ Minimizing Average WT!       │ Starvation of long burst jobs\nSRTF (Shortest Remaining Time) │ YES         │ Minimizing Average WT!       │ Starvation & high context switch overhead\nRound Robin (RR)               │ YES         │ Time-sharing / Response Time │ High context switches if quantum too small\nPriority Scheduling            │ Either      │ Mission-critical tasks       │ Starvation (Solved via Aging)\n</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SJF / SRTF gives the absolute theoretical MINIMUM average waiting time among all scheduling algorithms! In Round Robin, if Time Quantum q ≥ max(Burst Times), RR degenerates into FCFS.', 'number': '27.1', 'title': 'CPU Scheduling Algorithms & Gantt Chart Tracing'}, {'content': "• <b>The 4 Coffman Conditions (Must ALL hold simultaneously for Deadlock):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion:</b> Non-shareable resource mode.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Hold and Wait:</b> Process holds resources while requesting additional ones.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>No Preemption:</b> Resources cannot be forcibly seized.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Circular Wait:</b> Circular chain of processes where each holds a resource requested by next.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Banker's Algorithm (Deadlock Avoidance):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Need Matrix Formula:</b> <code>Need[i, j] = Max[i, j] - Allocation[i, j]</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Safety Check:</b> Find a process whose <code>Need ≤ Available</code>. Assume it finishes, release its allocated resources back to <code>Available = Available + Allocation</code>, and repeat.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• If all processes finish: State is <b>SAFE</b>! If not: State is <b>UNSAFE</b> (may lead to deadlock).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An UNSAFE state is NOT necessarily a Deadlock! Safe state guarantees NO deadlock. Unsafe state means deadlock is possible if worst-case requests occur.', 'number': '27.2', 'title': "Deadlock Characterization & Banker's Algorithm"}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Deadlock circular wait is a 4-way intersection where 4 cars turn right simultaneously: each car occupies one lane and waits for the next car to move, freezing traffic forever.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Four-Way Traffic Gridlock'}], 'title': 'CPU Scheduling, I/O Scheduling & Deadlocks', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Average Waiting Time = 5.33 ms.', 'problem': 'Processes P1, P2, P3 arrive at time 0 with burst times 10, 4, 2 ms. The Round Robin time quantum is 2 ms. Find the average waiting time.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Trace Gantt chart with time quantum = 2 ms:', '  [0-2: P1] (rem 8), [2-4: P2] (rem 2), [4-6: P3] (finishes at 6!),', '  [6-8: P1] (rem 6), [8-10: P2] (finishes at 10!),', '  [10-12: P1] (rem 4), [12-14: P1] (rem 2), [14-16: P1] (finishes at 16!).', 'Step 2: Completion Times: P3 = 6 ms, P2 = 10 ms, P1 = 16 ms.', 'Step 3: Turnaround Times (CT - AT): P1 = 16 - 0 = 16, P2 = 10 - 0 = 10, P3 = 6 - 0 = 6.', 'Step 4: Waiting Times (TAT - BT): P1 = 16 - 10 = 6, P2 = 10 - 4 = 6, P3 = 6 - 2 = 4.', 'Step 5: Average Waiting Time = (6 + 6 + 4) / 3 = 16 / 3 = 5.33 ms.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Round Robin Waiting Time Calculation'}]}, ('OS', 2): {'chapter_number': 28, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Page Table Size in Bytes vs Entries Trap', 'warning': 'Number of entries is 2^p. But Page Table SIZE in memory is 2^p × (PTE bytes)! For a 32-bit address space with 4 KB pages and 4-byte entries: Table size is 2²⁰ × 4 B = 4 MB.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Paging Architecture:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Logical Address broken into: <code>[ Page Number (p) | Page Offset (d) ]</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Page Size = Frame Size = <code>2ᵈ bytes</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Number of Pages = <code>Logical Address Space / Page Size</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Page Table Size:</b> <code>(Number of Pages) × (Page Table Entry Size)</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with TLB:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Let c = TLB lookup time, m = Main Memory access time, h = TLB hit ratio.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Formula:</b> <code>EMAT = h × (c + m) + (1 - h) × (c + 2m)</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- On TLB Hit: 1 TLB lookup + 1 memory access to fetch data.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- On TLB Miss: 1 TLB lookup + 1 memory access to read Page Table + 1 memory access to fetch data.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Two-Level Paging EMAT Formula:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>EMAT = h × (c + m) + (1 - h) × (c + 3m)</code> (3 memory reads: Outer Page Table, Inner Page Table, Actual Data).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In n-level paging, a TLB miss requires (n + 1) memory accesses! On a 4-level paging system (like x86-64), a TLB miss requires 5 memory accesses.', 'number': '28.1', 'title': 'Paging & Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with TLB'}, {'content': "• <b>Algorithms Compared:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>FIFO (First-In, First-Out):</b> Replaces oldest page loaded into memory.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Optimal (OPT / MIN):</b> Replaces page that will not be used for the longest period in the future (Theoretical benchmark; cannot be implemented in real OS).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>LRU (Least Recently Used):</b> Replaces page not used for the longest time in the past.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Belady's Anomaly:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- The bizarre phenomenon where <b>allocating MORE page frames causes MORE page faults</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Occurs in:</b> FIFO page replacement.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>CANNOT occur in:</b> Stack algorithms (like LRU and Optimal) because the set of pages in k frames is always a subset of pages in k+1 frames!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: LRU and Optimal algorithms NEVER suffer from Belady's Anomaly. FIFO can suffer from Belady's Anomaly on reference strings like: 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.", 'number': '28.2', 'title': "Page Replacement Algorithms & Belady's Anomaly"}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "Paging is like reading an encyclopedia: you don't load all 30 volumes onto your lap. You keep the 2 pages you are reading open on your desk and fetch new pages as needed.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Book Index & Virtual Memory'}], 'title': 'Memory Management, Virtual Memory & File Systems', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'EMAT = 130 ns.', 'problem': 'A system has a TLB hit ratio of 90%. TLB access time is 10 ns, and main memory access time is 100 ns. The system uses 2-level paging. Calculate the Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT).', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify parameters: h = 0.90, c = 10 ns, m = 100 ns, levels n = 2.', 'Step 2: On TLB Hit: Time = c + m = 10 + 100 = 110 ns.', 'Step 3: On TLB Miss: Time = c + (n + 1)m = 10 + 3(100) = 310 ns.', 'Step 4: Compute EMAT = h × (Hit Time) + (1 - h) × (Miss Time):', '  EMAT = 0.90 × 110 + 0.10 × 310 = 99 + 31 = 130 ns.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: EMAT Calculation with Multi-Level Paging'}]}, ('TOC', 0): {'chapter_number': 20, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Dead State Omission Trap in DFA Count', 'warning': 'In a DFA, EVERY state must have an outgoing transition for EVERY input symbol! Incomplete transitions must route to a Dead/Trap state. Do not forget to count the trap state in DFA state counts.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Formal Definitions of Finite Automata:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Both DFA and NFA are 5-tuples: <code>(Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DFA Transition:</b> <code>δ: Q × Σ → Q</code> (Deterministic: exactly 1 transition for every state-input pair).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>NFA Transition:</b> <code>δ: Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) → 2^Q</code> (Can transition to zero, one, or multiple states, or via ε-moves).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Equivalence & Power:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DFA and NFA have the EXACT SAME EXPRESSIVE POWER!</b> Both recognize exactly the class of <b>Regular Languages</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Subset Construction (Power Set Construction):</b> Converts an n-state NFA into an equivalent DFA.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Maximum states in equivalent DFA = <b>2ⁿ</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum states in equivalent DFA = <b>1</b> (e.g., if language is Σ*).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Standard NFA to DFA State Bounds Tested in GATE:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Language of strings where the <b>n-th symbol from the end</b> is 'a':<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- NFA requires: <code>n + 1</code> states.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimal DFA requires: strictly <b>2ⁿ states</b> (Exponential blowup!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Language of strings where the <b>n-th symbol from the beginning</b> is 'a':<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Both NFA and DFA require: <code>n + 2</code> states.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Minimal DFA for any regular language is 100% UNIQUE up to state renaming (isomorphism)! Minimal NFA is NOT necessarily unique.', 'number': '20.1', 'title': 'DFA vs NFA: Equivalences, Power & State Bounds'}, {'content': '• <b>Two States are Distinguishable:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Two states p and q are distinguishable if there exists some string w ∈ Σ* such that δ(p, w) ∈ F and δ(q, w) ∉ F (one state accepts, the other rejects).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Table-Filling Algorithm (Myhill-Nerode Theorem):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Remove all <b>unreachable states</b> from the start state q₀ (Dead code elimination).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Set up a triangular grid for all pairs {p, q}.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Base Step:</b> Mark {p, q} if one is accepting (F) and the other is non-accepting (Q \\ F).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Inductive Step:</b> For unmarked {p, q}, if for any input symbol a ∈ Σ the pair {δ(p, a), δ(q, a)} is already marked, then mark {p, q}!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;5. Repeat until no more marks are added. Unmarked pairs are <b>EQUIVALENT</b> and can be merged into a single state!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Myhill-Nerode Theorem states that a language L is regular if and only if the number of equivalence classes of its indistinguishability relation RL is FINITE. The number of equivalence classes equals the number of states in the minimal DFA!', 'number': '20.2', 'title': 'DFA Minimization: Myhill-Nerode & Table-Filling Method'}, {'content': "• <b>Closure Properties of Regular Languages:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Regular languages are <b>CLOSED UNDER ALL STANDARD OPERATIONS</b>:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Union, Intersection, Complement, Difference, Concatenation, Kleene Star (*), Reversal, Homomorphism, Inverse Homomorphism.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Decidability Matrix for Regular Languages:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>ALL questions are DECIDABLE for Regular Languages!</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Emptiness (L = ∅?):</b> Decidable (check if any final state is reachable from q₀ via BFS/DFS).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Finiteness (Is L finite?):</b> Decidable (check for cycles in paths leading to an accepting state).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Equivalence (L₁ = L₂?):</b> Decidable (construct DFA for (L₁ ∩ L₂') ∪ (L₁' ∩ L₂) and test if empty).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Universality (L = Σ*?):</b> Decidable (minimize DFA and check if it has 1 accepting state with self-loops).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If L₁ is Regular and L₂ is NOT Regular: L₁ ∪ L₂ may or may not be regular (e.g. Σ* ∪ non-regular = Σ*, which is regular). But L₁ ∩ L₂ is bounded by the regular set.', 'number': '20.3', 'title': 'Closure Properties & Decidability of Regular Languages'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'A vending machine is a finite automaton. Depositing coins transitions between states (10¢, 20¢, 50¢). Once the total reaches 50¢, the accepting state dispenses the beverage.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Vending Machine Controller'}], 'title': 'Finite Automata, Regular Expressions & Minimization', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Exactly 5 states in the minimal DFA.', 'problem': 'How many states are in the minimal DFA that accepts all binary strings whose decimal value is divisible by 5?', 'steps': ['Step 1: A binary number divisible by 5 has remainder r ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4} when divided by 5.', 'Step 2: Each distinct remainder modulo 5 represents a unique equivalence class in Myhill-Nerode relation.', 'Step 3: Define transition: Reading bit b transforms value V to 2V + b. Hence next state is (2r + b) mod 5.', 'Step 4: All 5 states (remainders 0, 1, 2, 3, 4) are reachable and pairwise distinguishable.', 'Step 5: The accepting state is remainder 0. Total states = 5.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Minimal DFA States for Modulo Counting'}]}, ('TOC', 1): {'chapter_number': 21, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Intersection of Two CFLs Trap', 'warning': 'L₁ = {aⁿ bⁿ cᵐ} is CFL. L₂ = {aᵐ bⁿ cⁿ} is CFL. But their intersection L₁ ∩ L₂ = {aⁿ bⁿ cⁿ} is NOT a CFL! CFLs are not closed under intersection.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Context-Free Grammar (CFG):</b> 4-tuple <code>(V, Σ, R, S)</code> where productions have single non-terminal on LHS: <code>A → α</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Ambiguity in Grammars:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• A grammar is <b>Ambiguous</b> if there exists at least one string w ∈ L(G) that has:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. More than one <b>Leftmost Derivation</b> (LMD), OR<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. More than one <b>Rightmost Derivation</b> (RMD), OR<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;3. More than one distinct <b>Parse Tree</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Inherent Ambiguity:</b> A CFL is <i>Inherently Ambiguous</i> if EVERY grammar generating it is ambiguous (e.g. L = {aⁿ bⁿ cᵐ} ∪ {aⁿ bᵐ cᵐ}).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Decidability:</b> Checking whether an arbitrary CFG is ambiguous is <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Classical ambiguous grammar: E → E + E | E * E | id. To remove ambiguity, introduce precedence levels (Terms for *, Factors for primary operands) and left-associativity.', 'number': '21.1', 'title': 'Context-Free Grammars (CFG) & Ambiguity'}, {'content': "• <b>PDA Mechanics:</b> Finite Automaton + 1 infinite Stack (LIFO memory).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>DPDA vs NPDA (CRITICAL DIFFERENCE):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DPDA (Deterministic PDA):</b> At any state, for any input and stack top, at most ONE move is possible. Recognizes <b>Deterministic Context-Free Languages (DCFL)</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>NPDA (Non-Deterministic PDA):</b> Multiple transitions allowed. Recognizes ALL <b>Context-Free Languages (CFL)</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Expressive Power:</b> <code>DCFL ⊂ CFL</code> (Strict subset! Unlike finite automata, NPDA is strictly MORE powerful than DPDA!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <i>Classic Example:</i><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Even palindrome with center marker <code>L = {w c wᴿ | w ∈ {a, b}*}</code> is in <b>DCFL</b> (DPDA knows when to reverse via 'c').<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Unmarked even palindrome <code>L = {w wᴿ | w ∈ {a, b}*}</code> is in <b>CFL but NOT DCFL</b> (requires non-deterministic guessing of midpoint!).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: DCFL is CLOSED under Complement! CFL is NOT closed under Complement or Intersection.', 'number': '21.2', 'title': 'Pushdown Automata: DPDA vs NPDA'}, {'content': '• <b>Pumping Lemma Statement for CFL:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;If L is a CFL, there exists a pumping length p such that any string s ∈ L with |s| ≥ p can be partitioned as <code>s = u v x y z</code> such that:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <code>|v y| ≥ 1</code> (At least one of v or y is non-empty).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <code>|v x y| ≤ p</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. For all i ≥ 0, <code>u vⁱ x yⁱ z ∈ L</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Classic Non-CFL Languages (Memorize for GATE):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>L = {aⁿ bⁿ cⁿ | n ≥ 1}</code> (NOT a CFL - requires matching 3 counts simultaneously).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>L = {aⁿ bᵐ cⁿ dᵐ}</code> (NOT a CFL - interleaved dependencies).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>L = {w w | w ∈ {a, b}*}</code> (NOT a CFL - requires queue, not stack).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>L = {a^(p) | p is a prime}</code> (NOT a CFL).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A stack can only compare TWO counts simultaneously (e.g. {aⁿ bⁿ}). To compare 3 counts ({aⁿ bⁿ cⁿ}), you need a Turing Machine!', 'number': '21.3', 'title': 'Pumping Lemma for CFLs & Non-CFL Proofs'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Validating balanced HTML tags (<div><p></p></div>) is a context-free grammar handled by a PDA stack. Every opening tag is pushed and matched by a closing tag.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: HTML Tag Matching'}], 'title': 'Grammars, Pushdown Automata & Pumping Lemma', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'L is a DCFL (Deterministic Context-Free Language).', 'problem': 'Identify the type of language: L = {aⁿ bⁿ cᵐ dᵐ | n, m ≥ 1}. Is it Regular, DCFL, or non-CFL?', 'steps': ["Step 1: Notice the structure: a block of a's followed by b's (with equal counts n), then c's followed by d's (with equal counts m).", "Step 2: A PDA can push a's on stack, pop a's against b's until stack is empty. Then push c's on stack, and pop c's against d's.", 'Step 3: Since there is no backtracking or guessing required, a single deterministic stack can recognize it.', 'Step 4: It cannot be regular because n and m are unbounded (requires counting).', 'Step 5: Therefore, L is a Deterministic Context-Free Language (DCFL).'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Classifying Language L = {aⁿ bⁿ cᵐ dᵐ}'}]}, ('TOC', 2): {'chapter_number': 22, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Complement of RE Languages Trap', 'warning': 'If language L is RE and its complement ~L is ALSO RE, then L is guaranteed to be RECURSIVE (Decidable)! If L is RE but undecidable (like Halting Problem), its complement ~L is strictly NOT RE.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Chomsky Hierarchy Master Classification:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nType   │ Language Class                   │ Automaton Machine                 │ Production Grammar Rules\n──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nType-3 │ Regular                          │ Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA)│ A → aB or A → a (Right linear)\nType-2 │ Context-Free (CFL)               │ Non-Deterministic Pushdown (NPDA) │ A → α (Single non-terminal LHS)\nType-1 │ Context-Sensitive (CSL)          │ Linear Bounded Automata (LBA)     │ α → β with |α| ≤ |β| (Non-contracting)\nType-0 │ Recursively Enumerable (RE)      │ Turing Machine (TM)               │ α → β (No restrictions)\n</div>\n\n• <b>Subsets Inclusion:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ Recursive (REC) ⊂ Recursively Enumerable (RE)</code>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Recursive (REC / Turing Decidable) languages are HALTING Turing Machines (always halts with Yes or No). Recursively Enumerable (RE / Turing Recognizable) may loop infinitely on negative inputs!', 'number': '22.1', 'title': 'The Chomsky Hierarchy of Languages'}, {'content': "• <b>What is a Decidable Problem?</b> A problem is decidable if there exists an algorithm (Turing machine that halts on ALL inputs) that answers Yes or No correctly.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Turing Halting Problem (HP):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>'Given an arbitrary program M and input w, does M halt on w?'</i><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Proven <b>UNDECIDABLE</b> by Alan Turing using Cantor's Diagonalization Argument!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• It is <b>Semi-Decidable (RE)</b>, but NOT Decidable.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Rice's Theorem (GATE Holy Grail for Undecidability):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>'Any non-trivial semantic property of the language recognized by a Turing Machine is UNDECIDABLE!'</i><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Semantic Property:</b> A property of the LANGUAGE L(M) recognized (e.g., Is L(M) empty? Is L(M) finite? Is L(M) regular?).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Non-trivial:</b> Holds for some TMs and does not hold for others.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Rice's Theorem Verdict:</b> ALL such questions: 'Is L(M) empty?', 'Is L(M) = Σ*?', 'Is L(M) regular?' are strictly <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Rice's Theorem applies ONLY to semantic properties of the language L(M)! Syntactic properties of the machine itself (e.g. 'Does TM M have 5 states?', 'Does M take >10 steps on input w?') are DECIDABLE.", 'number': '22.2', 'title': 'Turing Halting Problem & Undecidability'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Writing an antivirus software that can guarantee whether ANY arbitrary computer program will ever freeze or terminate is mathematically impossible due to the Turing Halting Problem.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Antivirus Infinite Loop Checker'}], 'title': 'Turing Machines, Chomsky Hierarchy & Undecidability', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'The problem is UNDECIDABLE.', 'problem': 'Determine whether the following problem is Decidable: Given a Turing Machine M, is the language accepted by M, L(M), a regular language?', 'steps': ["Step 1: Check if this is a property of the language L(M). Yes, 'is L(M) regular' depends on the language accepted, not the machine description.", 'Step 2: Check if the property is non-trivial: (a) There exist TMs whose language is regular (e.g., L(M) = {a}*). (b) There exist TMs whose language is not regular (e.g., L(M) = {aⁿ bⁿ}).', "Step 3: Since it is a non-trivial semantic property of the language of a TM, apply Rice's Theorem directly.", "Step 4: By Rice's Theorem, the problem is UNDECIDABLE."], 'title': "Worked Example: Applying Rice's Theorem"}]}, ('CN', 0): {'chapter_number': 32, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Bits vs Bytes Unit Conversion Trap', 'warning': 'Packet size is usually given in Bytes (KB), but Bandwidth is given in bits/sec (Mbps). ALWAYS multiply bytes by 8 to convert to bits before computing T_t = L / B!'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>The 7 OSI Layers & Their Data Units:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nOSI Layer           │ Protocol Data Unit (PDU) │ Core Addressing Used          │ Hardware Devices\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nApplication (7-5)   │ Message / Data           │ Process / Domain Name (DNS)   │ Gateway\nTransport (4)       │ Segment (TCP) / Datagram │ Port Number (16-bit)          │ Firewall / Load Balancer\nNetwork (3)         │ Packet                   │ IP Address (32 / 128-bit)     │ Router / L3 Switch\nData Link (2)       │ Frame                    │ MAC Address (48-bit)          │ Bridge / L2 Switch / NIC\nPhysical (1)        │ Bits                     │ Physical signaling (Volts, Hz)│ Hub / Repeater\n</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Hub and Repeater work at Layer 1 (Physical). Bridge and Switch work at Layer 2 (Data Link - Collision domains separated). Router works at Layer 3 (Network - Broadcast domains separated).', 'number': '32.1', 'title': 'OSI 7-Layer vs TCP/IP Models & Protocol Stacks'}, {'content': "• <b>The 4 Fundamental Network Delays:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Transmission Delay (Tₜ):</b> Time to push all packet bits onto the wire: <code>Tₜ = Packet Size (L) / Bandwidth (B)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Propagation Delay (Tₚ):</b> Time for 1 bit to travel from sender to receiver: <code>Tₚ = Distance (d) / Propagation Speed (v)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Queuing Delay:</b> Time waiting in router buffers.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Processing Delay:</b> Time taken by router to examine packet headers.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Bandwidth-Delay Product (BDP):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>BDP = Bandwidth × Propagation Delay</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Represents the maximum volume of bits that can be 'in flight' on the transmission link at any given instant!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Transmission delay depends on bandwidth (bits/sec), NOT distance! Propagation delay depends on physical distance and medium speed, NOT bandwidth.', 'number': '32.2', 'title': 'Network Delays & Bandwidth-Delay Product (BDP)'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Transmission delay is how fast you pour water into a pipe. Propagation delay is how long the first drop takes to travel through the pipe to the other end.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Pipe Length vs Flow Rate'}], 'title': 'Layering, Switching & Network Performance Metrics', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Transmission Delay = 0.8 ms, Propagation Delay = 10 ms.', 'problem': 'A 1000-byte packet is sent over a 10 Mbps point-to-point link of distance 2000 km. The propagation speed is 2 × 10⁸ m/s. Find the transmission and propagation delays.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Transmission delay T_t = L / B = (1000 × 8 bits) / (10 × 10⁶ bps) = 8000 / 10,000,000 = 0.0008 s = 0.8 ms.', 'Step 2: Propagation delay T_p = d / v = (2000 × 10³ m) / (2 × 10⁸ m/s) = 2,000,000 / 200,000,000 = 0.01 s = 10 ms.', 'Step 3: Notice: T_p (10 ms) is over 12 times larger than T_t (0.8 ms)! The link is propagation-dominated.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Transmission Delay vs Propagation Delay'}]}, ('CN', 1): {'chapter_number': 33, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Round Trip Time Factor 2 Trap', 'warning': 'In CSMA/CD, the factor of 2 (2 × T_p) accounts for the WORST-CASE round-trip collision scenario where collision occurs just before reaching the destination.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Let parameter 'a':</b> <code>a = Propagation Delay (Tₚ) / Transmission Delay (Tₜ)</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Master Comparison of Flow Control Protocols:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nProtocol           │ Sender Window (Wₛ) │ Receiver Window (Wᵣ) │ Efficiency (η) Formula        │ Minimum Sequence Bits\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nStop-and-Wait      │ 1                  │ 1                    │ η = 1 / (1 + 2a)              │ 1 bit (Seq 0 and 1)\nGo-Back-N (GBN)    │ N                  │ 1                    │ η = N / (1 + 2a) (max 100%)   │ ⌈log₂(N + 1)⌉ bits\nSelective Repeat   │ N                  │ N                    │ η = N / (1 + 2a) (max 100%)   │ ⌈log₂(2N)⌉ bits\n</div>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Selective Repeat, Sender and Receiver window sizes must satisfy: W_s + W_r ≤ 2^k (where k is number of sequence bits). With W_s = W_r = N: N ≤ 2^(k-1)!', 'number': '33.1', 'title': 'Sliding Window Protocol Efficiency & Window Sizes'}, {'content': '• <b>Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection (CSMA/CD):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Used in classic half-duplex Ethernet.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>The Fundamental Collision Detection Condition:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;To detect a collision before transmission completes, the transmission time MUST be at least twice the propagation delay!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>Tₜ ≥ 2 × Tₚ</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>(L_min / Bandwidth) ≥ 2 × (Distance / v)</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>L_min = 2 × Bandwidth × Tₚ</code>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a packet is smaller than L_min, sender finishes transmitting and assumes success before the collision signal can travel back! This is why Ethernet has a mandatory minimum frame size of 64 bytes.', 'number': '33.2', 'title': 'CSMA/CD & Minimum Frame Size Condition'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'CSMA/CD is a room of polite people: listen before speaking (Carrier Sense); if two people start talking at once, stop immediately and wait a random delay (Exponential Backoff).', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Polite Group Discussion'}], 'title': 'Data Link Layer, CRC & Medium Access Control (CSMA/CD)', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Minimum frame size is 1250 bytes (10,000 bits).', 'problem': 'A 1 Gbps CSMA/CD network has a cable length of 1 km with signal propagation speed 2 × 10⁸ m/s. What is the minimum frame size required for reliable collision detection?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate propagation delay T_p = d / v = 1000 m / (2 × 10⁸ m/s) = 5 × 10⁻⁶ s = 5 µs.', 'Step 2: Apply the CSMA/CD condition: T_t ≥ 2 × T_p ⇒ T_t_min = 2 × 5 µs = 10 µs.', 'Step 3: Calculate minimum frame length L_min = Bandwidth × T_t_min = (1 × 10⁹ bps) × (10 × 10⁻⁶ s) = 10,000 bits.', 'Step 4: Convert to bytes: 10,000 / 8 = 1250 bytes.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Minimum Frame Size in CSMA/CD'}]}, ('CN', 2): {'chapter_number': 34, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'HLEN 4-Byte Scaling vs Offset 8-Byte Scaling Trap', 'warning': 'HLEN is scaled by 4 bytes (e.g. HLEN=6 means 24 bytes). Fragment Offset is scaled by 8 bytes (e.g. Offset=100 means byte 800). Never mix up these two scaling factors.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>CIDR Notation (/n):</b> IP address format <code>a.b.c.d / n</code> where n is the number of Network Prefix bits.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Host bits (h):</b> <code>h = 32 - n</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Total IP Addresses in Subnet:</b> <code>2ʰ</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Usable Host Addresses:</b> <code>2ʰ - 2</code> (Subtract 2: Network ID with all 0 host bits, and Broadcast ID with all 1 host bits).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Subnetting Example (/26):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;h = 32 - 26 = 6 bits. Total IPs = 2⁶ = 64. Usable hosts = 64 - 2 = 62 hosts per subnet.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Longest Prefix Match Rule: When a router receives a packet, it compares the destination IP against all routing table entries. If multiple routes match, the router strictly picks the route with the LONGEST prefix (highest /n value)!', 'number': '34.1', 'title': 'IPv4 Subnetting, CIDR & Usable Hosts'}, {'content': "• <b>IPv4 Header Fields (Tested in GATE):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Header Length (HLEN):</b> 4-bit field, scaled in <b>4-byte words</b>! Minimum HLEN = 5 (20 bytes); Maximum = 15 (60 bytes).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Total Length:</b> 16 bits (max datagram size = 65,535 bytes).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Fragmentation Offset:</b> 13 bits, measured in <b>units of 8 bytes</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Flags:</b> [ Reserved | DF (Don't Fragment) | MF (More Fragments) ].<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If MF = 1: More fragments follow.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If MF = 0: Last fragment (or unfragmented packet).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The fragmentation offset field stores: (Original Data Byte Offset) / 8! The payload size of every fragment (except the last) MUST be a multiple of 8 bytes.', 'number': '34.2', 'title': 'IPv4 Header & Packet Fragmentation'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'NAT is the receptionist in an office: your laptop, phone, and TV share 1 public IP address, and the NAT table routes incoming responses to your device via internal port numbers.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: NAT Router & Home Wi-Fi'}], 'title': 'Routing Protocols & IPv4 Addressing (CIDR, Fragmentation, NAT)', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Fragment 2: Payload = 1480 bytes, MF = 1, Offset = 185.', 'problem': 'An IPv4 datagram of 4000 bytes (20 bytes header + 3980 bytes payload) arrives at a router whose outgoing link has an MTU of 1500 bytes. Find the payload size, MF flag, and Offset of the second fragment.', 'steps': ['Step 1: MTU = 1500 bytes. Including 20 bytes IP header, maximum data payload per fragment = 1500 - 20 = 1480 bytes.', 'Step 2: Check multiple of 8: 1480 / 8 = 185 (perfect multiple of 8!).', 'Step 3: Fragment 1: Payload = 1480 bytes (bytes 0 to 1479). MF = 1. Offset = 0 / 8 = 0.', 'Step 4: Fragment 2: Takes next 1480 bytes (bytes 1480 to 2959). MF = 1. Offset = 1480 / 8 = 185.', 'Step 5: Fragment 3: Remaining data = 3980 - 1480 - 1480 = 1020 bytes (bytes 2960 to 3979). MF = 0. Offset = 2960 / 8 = 370.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: IPv4 Packet Fragmentation Trace'}]}, ('CN', 3): {'chapter_number': 35, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'TCP Acknowledgment Number Trap', 'warning': "TCP ACK is cumulative: Ack = N means 'I have received all bytes up to N - 1 successfully, and I am expecting byte N next'."}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>TCP 3-Way Handshake:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Client → Server: <code>SYN, Seq = x</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Server → Client: <code>SYN + ACK, Seq = y, Ack = x + 1</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Client → Server: <code>ACK, Seq = x + 1, Ack = y + 1</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>TCP Congestion Control Phases:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Slow Start:</b> <code>cwnd</code> starts at 1 MSS and <b>DOUBLES every RTT</b> (Exponential growth: 1, 2, 4, 8...).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Congestion Avoidance:</b> When <code>cwnd ≥ Threshold (ssthresh)</code>, growth becomes linear: <b>increases by 1 MSS every RTT</b> (Additive Increase).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>On Timeout (Severe Congestion):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>cwnd = 1 MSS</code> (Resets back to slow start!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>On 3 Duplicate ACKs (TCP Tahoe vs Reno):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Tahoe: cwnd resets to 1 MSS.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Reno (Fast Recovery): <code>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</code>, and <code>cwnd = ssthresh</code> (Skips slow start, continues linear growth!).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Sequence number consumes 1 number for SYN and FIN flags! ACK packets that carry no data do NOT consume sequence numbers.', 'number': '35.1', 'title': 'TCP 3-Way Handshake & Congestion Control'}, {'content': "• <b>DNS (Domain Name System - Port 53):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Uses <b>UDP</b> for standard client queries (fast, low overhead). Uses <b>TCP</b> for zone transfers.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Iterative Query:</b> Server replies with the referral address of next server. Client does the walking.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Recursive Query:</b> Server resolves the full name on client's behalf and returns final IP.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>HTTP Protocols:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>HTTP/1.0:</b> Non-persistent (1 TCP handshake per image/file).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>HTTP/1.1:</b> Persistent connections (re-uses TCP connection; suffers from Head-of-Line blocking).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>HTTP/2:</b> Binary framing, multiplexing multiple streams over a single TCP connection.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Persistent HTTP without pipelining takes 1 RTT per referenced object. Persistent HTTP WITH pipelining takes 1 RTT total for all referenced objects.', 'number': '35.2', 'title': 'Application Layer: DNS Resolution & HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'TCP slow start rapidly expands your YouTube video buffer during initial loading, then stabilizes into linear mode once smooth 4K playback is achieved.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Video Streaming Buffer'}], 'title': 'Transport Layer (TCP), Sockets & Application Protocols (DNS, HTTP)', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'cwnd in round 7 is 18 MSS.', 'problem': 'A TCP connection has ssthresh = 16 MSS. In round 1, cwnd = 1 MSS. Assuming no packet loss occurs, what is the size of cwnd in round 7?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Round 1: cwnd = 1 MSS (Slow start).', 'Step 2: Round 2: cwnd = 2 MSS.', 'Step 3: Round 3: cwnd = 4 MSS.', 'Step 4: Round 4: cwnd = 8 MSS.', 'Step 5: Round 5: cwnd = 16 MSS (Hits ssthresh = 16! Switches to Congestion Avoidance).', 'Step 6: Round 6: cwnd = 16 + 1 = 17 MSS (Linear growth).', 'Step 7: Round 7: cwnd = 17 + 1 = 18 MSS.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: TCP Congestion Window Calculation'}]}, ('DB', 0): {'chapter_number': 29, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'SQL NULL Comparison with Equality Trap', 'warning': "In SQL, comparing anything to NULL with '=' (e.g. 'salary = NULL') evaluates to UNKNOWN, never TRUE! Always use 'IS NULL' or 'IS NOT NULL'."}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Cardinality Ratios & Table Merging Rules:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nRelationship Type │ Cardinality / Participation        │ Minimum Relational Tables Needed\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nMany-to-Many (M:N)│ Any participation                  │ 3 Tables (Table A, Table B, Table AB)\nOne-to-Many (1:N) │ Any participation                  │ 2 Tables (Foreign key placed on the 'MANY' side!)\nOne-to-One (1:1)  │ Partial on both sides (0..1, 0..1) │ 2 Tables (Foreign key on either side with UNIQUE constraint)\nOne-to-One (1:1)  │ Total on one side (1..1, 0..1)     │ 2 Tables (Foreign key placed on the TOTAL participation side!)\nOne-to-One (1:1)  │ Total on BOTH sides (1..1, 1..1)   │ 1 SINGLE MERGED TABLE (Combines both entities!)\n</div>\n\n• <b>Weak Entity Sets:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Does not have sufficient attributes to form a primary key.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Depends on an <b>Identifying Strong Entity</b> via an <b>Identifying Relationship</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Primary Key of Weak Entity:</b> <code>Primary Key of Strong Entity + Discriminator (Partial Key)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Weak entity and its identifying relationship are ALWAYS merged into a single table!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 1:N relationship, NEVER place the foreign key on the 1-side (causes massive NULL or multivalued redundancy)! Always place the foreign key on the N-side.', 'number': '29.1', 'title': 'ER to Relational Table Conversion Rules (Minimal Tables)'}, {'content': "• <b>Core Relational Algebra Operators:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selection (σ_condition):</b> Filters rows (horizontal subset).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Projection (π_attributes):</b> Selects columns (vertical subset; eliminates duplicates).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Cross Product (R × S):</b> Combines every tuple of R with every tuple of S.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Natural Join (R ⋈ S):</b> Equi-join on all shared common attributes, eliminating redundant duplicate columns.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>The Division Operator (R ÷ S):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Used to answer queries containing the word <b>'FOR ALL'</b> (e.g. <i>'Find students who have enrolled in ALL courses offered by Prof. Rao'</i>).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Result schema: Attributes in R that are NOT in S: <code>Schema(R ÷ S) = Schema(R) - Schema(S)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Formal formula: <code>R ÷ S = π_{R-S}(R) - π_{R-S}( (π_{R-S}(R) × S) - R )</code>.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Tuple Relational Calculus (TRC) and Domain Relational Calculus (DRC) are equivalent in expressive power to Relational Algebra (Codd's Theorem). SQL is declarative, based directly on TRC.", 'number': '29.2', 'title': 'Relational Algebra: Joins, Division & Calculus'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "A university database uses M:N relationship tables: Students and Courses connect through an 'Enrollment' bridge table that stores grades and semester dates.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: University Enrollment Database'}], 'title': 'ER-Model, Relational Algebra & SQL', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Minimum 2 tables required.', 'problem': 'An ER diagram has entities Student and Department. A student belongs to at most one department, and a department can have multiple students. Both have partial participation. How many minimum relational tables are needed?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify cardinality ratio: Department-to-Student is One-to-Many (1:N).', "Step 2: Recall the 1:N table conversion rule: We can merge the relationship table into the entity table on the 'MANY' side (Student).", 'Step 3: Table 1: Department(Dept_ID, Dept_Name) with Dept_ID as Primary Key.', 'Step 4: Table 2: Student(Roll_No, Name, Dept_ID) with Roll_No as Primary Key, and Dept_ID as Foreign Key referencing Department.', 'Step 5: Minimum tables required = 2.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Minimum Relational Tables from ER Diagram'}]}, ('DB', 1): {'chapter_number': 30, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'All-Prime Attributes Guarantee 3NF Trap', 'warning': 'If all attributes in a relation are Prime Attributes, the relation is GUARANTEED to be in 3NF! However, it may still violate BCNF.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Attribute Closure (X⁺):</b> The set of all attributes functionally determined by attribute set X.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Step-by-Step Candidate Key Finding Algorithm:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Essential Attributes (Must be in every Candidate Key):</b> Attributes that appear ONLY on the LHS of dependencies, or never appear in any dependency.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Non-Essential Attributes:</b> Attributes that appear ONLY on the RHS can NEVER be part of any minimal candidate key!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Compute closure of essential attributes. If it derives all attributes, it is the <b>UNIQUE Candidate Key</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. If not, combine with intermediate attributes until closure covers the entire relation schema.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Prime Attribute: Any attribute that is a part of AT LEAST ONE Candidate Key. Non-Prime Attribute: An attribute that does NOT belong to ANY Candidate Key.', 'number': '30.1', 'title': 'Candidate Keys & Attribute Closure Algorithm'}, {'content': "• <b>Hierarchical Normal Forms Decision Checklist:</b><br/>\n<div class='font-mono text-xs p-3 bg-slate-900 text-slate-100 rounded-xl my-2 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto'>\nNormal Form │ Violation Condition to Check For                               │ Required Standard\n────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n1NF         │ Multi-valued or composite attributes exist                      │ All attribute values are ATOMIC\n2NF         │ Partial Dependency: Proper Subset of Key → Non-Prime Attribute  │ NO Partial Dependencies!\n3NF         │ Transitive Dependency: Non-Prime → Non-Prime Attribute          │ For every X → Y: X is Superkey OR Y is Prime\nBCNF        │ Any functional dependency where LHS is not a Superkey           │ For EVERY X → Y: X MUST be a Superkey!\n</div>\n\n• <b>Decomposition Properties (Lossless vs Dependency Preserving):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Lossless Join Condition:</b> R₁ ∩ R₂ → R₁ OR R₁ ∩ R₂ → R₂ (The common attribute must be a Superkey in at least one table!).<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Decomposition into 3NF:</b> Always guarantees BOTH Lossless Join AND Dependency Preservation!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Decomposition into BCNF:</b> Always guarantees Lossless Join, but <b>MAY NOT PRESERVE DEPENDENCIES</b>!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any binary decomposition into two tables R₁ and R₂ is LOSSLESS if and only if their common attribute (R₁ ∩ R₂) forms a candidate key of R₁ or R₂!', 'number': '30.2', 'title': 'The Step-by-Step Normal Forms Checklist (1NF to BCNF)'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "Normalization eliminates anomalies: if a customer's address is stored in every invoice row, updating their address requires updating 1,000 rows (update anomaly) or risks inconsistent data.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Eliminating Redundant Billing Data'}], 'title': 'Functional Dependencies & Normalization (1NF to BCNF)', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'The relation R is in 3NF, but NOT in BCNF.', 'problem': 'Given relation R(A, B, C, D) with FDs: {AB → C, C → D, D → A}. What is the highest normal form of R?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Find candidate keys. Compute closures: (AB)⁺ = {A, B, C, D}. Since C → D and D → A: (BC)⁺ = {B, C, D, A}. (BD)⁺ = {B, D, A, C}.', 'Step 2: Candidate Keys are AB, BC, BD.', 'Step 3: Prime attributes = {A, B, C, D}. ALL attributes are prime!', 'Step 4: Check 2NF: No non-prime attributes exist, so no partial dependencies. Satisfies 2NF.', 'Step 5: Check 3NF: In every FD X → Y, RHS (Y) is a prime attribute! Since RHS is prime, 3NF condition is satisfied for ALL FDs. Satisfies 3NF.', 'Step 6: Check BCNF: For C → D, C is not a superkey! For D → A, D is not a superkey! Hence, BCNF is VIOLATED.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Highest Normal Form Identification'}]}, ('DB', 2): {'chapter_number': 31, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Two-Phase Locking (2PL) vs Deadlock Trap', 'warning': 'Strict 2PL guarantees Conflict Serializability and prevents Cascading Aborts, but IT DOES NOT PREVENT DEADLOCKS! Systems using 2PL can still deadlock.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Key Structural Differences:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>B-Tree:</b> Both Internal nodes and Leaf nodes store data records / record pointers.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>B+ Tree:</b> Internal nodes store ONLY search keys and child tree pointers. <b>ALL data records / record pointers are stored strictly in the LEAF nodes!</b> Leaf nodes are linked as a Doubly Linked List for blazing fast range queries.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>B+ Tree Node Order (p) Sizing Equations:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;Given: Block Size = B bytes, Key Size = K bytes, Record Pointer = R bytes, Block/Child Pointer = P bytes.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Internal Node Equation:</b> <code>p · P + (p - 1) · K ≤ B</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Leaf Node Equation:</b> <code>q · (K + R) + P ≤ B</code> (where P is the sibling leaf pointer).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a B+ tree of order p: Root has at least 2 children. Internal nodes have at least ⌈p / 2⌉ children. Leaves have at least ⌈(p - 1) / 2⌉ keys.', 'number': '31.1', 'title': 'B-Tree vs B+ Tree Node Capacity Calculations'}, {'content': '• <b>Conflict Operations:</b> Two operations on the same data item by different transactions where <b>at least ONE operation is a WRITE</b> (R-W, W-R, W-W).<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Precedence Graph (Testing Conflict Serializability):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Create a vertex for each transaction Tᵢ.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Draw directed edge Tᵢ → Tⱼ if an operation of Tᵢ precedes a conflicting operation of Tⱼ.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>The Serializability Theorem:</b> A schedule is Conflict Serializable if and only if the <b>Precedence Graph is ACYCLIC (contains NO cycles)</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;4. The equivalent serial schedule order is obtained via <b>Topological Sort</b> of the precedence graph.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Conflict Serializable ⇒ View Serializable. But View Serializable does NOT imply Conflict Serializable! View serializable schedules with cycles require Blind Writes.', 'number': '31.2', 'title': 'Conflict Serializability & Precedence Graphs'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'ACID Atomicity ensures that if the ATM cash dispenser jams after your bank balance is deducted, the entire transaction rolls back automatically so you never lose money.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: ATM Cash Withdrawal & ACID'}], 'title': 'B/B+ Trees, Transactions & Concurrency Control', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Maximum order p = 57 child pointers.', 'problem': 'A disk block size is 1024 bytes. Data keys are 12 bytes, child tree pointers are 6 bytes. Find the maximum order (fan-out) p of an internal node in a B+ tree.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for internal node: p · P + (p - 1) · K ≤ Block Size.', 'Step 2: Substitute values: p(6) + (p - 1)(12) ≤ 1024.', 'Step 3: Simplify: 6p + 12p - 12 ≤ 1024 ⇒ 18p ≤ 1036.', 'Step 4: Solve for p: p ≤ 1036 / 18 ≈ 57.55.', 'Step 5: Since order must be an integer: p = 57.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: B+ Tree Internal Node Order'}]}, ('GA', 0): {'chapter_number': 36, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'The False Friend Relative Pronoun Trap', 'warning': "'One of the students who HAVE passed' vs 'The ONLY one of the students who HAS passed'. In 'one of those who', the antecedent is the plural noun!"}], 'foundations': [{'content': "• <b>Fundamental Concord Rule:</b> A singular subject takes a singular verb; a plural subject takes a plural verb.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>High-Yield GATE Grammar Rules:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Rule of Proximity (Neither... Nor, Either... Or):</b> The verb agrees with the subject CLOSER to it!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>'Neither the manager nor the <u>employees were</u> present.'</i><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>'Neither the employees nor the <u>manager was</u> present.'</i><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Intervening Phrases:</b> Phrases like <i>'as well as'</i>, <i>'along with'</i>, <i>'together with'</i> DO NOT change subject number!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <i>'The President, along with his advisors, <b>is</b> arriving.'</i> (Subject is singular 'President').<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Collective Nouns:</b> Singular when acting as a unified unit (<i>'The committee <b>has</b> reached a consensus'</i>); Plural when acting individually (<i>'The committee <b>are</b> divided in their opinions'</i>).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In sentences beginning with 'Each of...', 'Either of...', 'Neither of...', the subject is strictly SINGULAR! Always use singular verb: 'Each of the students IS prepared'.", 'number': '36.1', 'title': 'Subject-Verb Agreement (Concord) Rules'}, {'content': "• <b>Definitions:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Fact:</b> Explicitly stated truth in the passage.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Inference:</b> A logical deduction that is 100% guaranteed to be true based on the passage.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Assumption:</b> An UNSTATED premise that the author MUST believe to be true for the conclusion to stand.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>The Negation Test for Assumptions:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- To verify if an option is a valid assumption: <b>Negate the option</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- If negating the option <b>DESTROYS or INVALIDATES the author's argument</b>, that option is the correct mandatory assumption!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In reading comprehension, beware of extreme words like 'always', 'never', 'all', 'none'. Unless explicitly supported by the text, extreme answer choices are almost always traps.", 'number': '36.2', 'title': 'Critical Reasoning: Assumptions, Inferences & Conclusions'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': "Critical reasoning is courtroom law: an inference is proven forensic evidence; an assumption is an unproven hypothesis that must hold for the prosecution's case to make sense.", 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Courtroom Evidence vs Hearsay'}], 'title': 'Verbal Aptitude - Grammar, Vocabulary & Comprehension', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': "Correct verb: 'lends'", 'problem': "Choose the grammatically correct option: 'A bouquet of yellow roses _____ (lend / lends) a vibrant color to the room.'", 'steps': ["Step 1: Identify the grammatical subject. The prepositional phrase is 'of yellow roses'.", "Step 2: The actual head noun of the subject is 'A bouquet' (Singular).", "Step 3: A singular subject requires a singular verb with an 's' ending ('lends').", "Step 4: The plural noun 'roses' is an object of the preposition, not the subject."], 'title': 'Worked Example: Subject-Verb Agreement Selection'}]}, ('GA', 1): {'chapter_number': 37, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Average Speed Arithmetic Mean Trap', 'warning': 'Traveling distance d at 40 km/hr and returning at 60 km/hr gives average speed 2(40)(60)/(40+60) = 48 km/hr, NOT 50 km/hr! Arithmetic mean is strictly wrong.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Fundamental Formula:</b> <code>Distance = Speed × Time</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Conversion: <code>1 km/hr = 5 / 18 m/s</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Relative Speed Mechanics:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>OPPOSITE</b> directions: <code>S_rel = S₁ + S₂</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in the <b>SAME</b> direction: <code>S_rel = |S₁ - S₂|</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Boats and Streams:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Let boat speed in still water = u, stream speed = v.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Downstream speed: <code>D = u + v</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Upstream speed: <code>U = u - v</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>u = (D + U) / 2</code>, <code>v = (D - U) / 2</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Average Speed for Equal Distances:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>S_avg = (2 · S₁ · S₂) / (S₁ + S₂)</code> (Harmonic Mean, NOT Arithmetic Mean!).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a train of length L passes a standing person/pole, distance covered is L. If it passes a platform/bridge of length P, distance covered is L + P!', 'number': '37.1', 'title': 'Time, Speed, Distance & Relative Speed'}, {'content': "• <b>Work-Rate Equivalence:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;If person A finishes work in X days, A's 1-day work rate = <code>1 / X</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Combined rate of A and B: <code>1/X + 1/Y = (X + Y) / (XY)</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Time taken together: <code>T = (X · Y) / (X + Y)</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>LCM Method (Fastest Exam Technique):</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Assume Total Work = LCM of individual days.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Calculate individual daily efficiency = Total Work / Individual days.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Total Time = Total Work / Combined Efficiency.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Pipes and Cisterns, an inlet pipe has positive efficiency (+E), while an outlet/leak pipe has negative efficiency (-E)!', 'number': '37.2', 'title': 'Work and Time & Pipes and Cisterns'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Work and time calculations mirror software sprint velocity: 3 developers finishing 15 story points together allows planning parallel delivery schedules.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Project Sprints in Tech'}], 'title': 'Quantitative Aptitude - Arithmetic, Algebra & Mensuration', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Train speeds are 30 m/s and 20 m/s (108 km/hr and 72 km/hr).', 'problem': 'Two trains of length 150 m and 250 m run on parallel tracks. When running in the same direction, the faster train passes the slower in 40 seconds. When running in opposite directions, they pass each other in 8 seconds. Find the speeds of both trains.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Total distance covered in passing = Sum of lengths = 150 + 250 = 400 m.', 'Step 2: Same direction relative speed S₁ - S₂ = Distance / Time = 400 m / 40 s = 10 m/s.', 'Step 3: Opposite direction relative speed S₁ + S₂ = Distance / Time = 400 m / 8 s = 50 m/s.', 'Step 4: Solve system: (S₁ + S₂) + (S₁ - S₂) = 50 + 10 ⇒ 2S₁ = 60 ⇒ S₁ = 30 m/s (108 km/hr).', 'Step 5: S₂ = 50 - 30 = 20 m/s (72 km/hr).'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Relative Speed Train Crossing'}]}, ('GA', 2): {'chapter_number': 38, 'exam_traps': [{'title': 'Reflex Angle vs Interior Angle Trap', 'warning': 'If the question asks for the angle between hands, the standard answer is the interior angle (≤ 180°). If the formula gives > 180°, subtract from 360°.'}], 'foundations': [{'content': '• <b>Clock Angle Formula:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;The angle θ between Hour hand and Minute hand at H hours and M minutes is:<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;<code>θ = | 30H - (11 / 2) M |</code><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Speed of Minute hand: <code>6° per minute</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Speed of Hour hand: <code>0.5° per minute</code>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Relative speed of Minute hand over Hour hand: <code>5.5° = (11 / 2)° per minute</code>.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Calendar Odd Days:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Ordinary Year (365 days): 52 weeks + <b>1 odd day</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Leap Year (366 days): 52 weeks + <b>2 odd days</b>.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• Centenary Years: Leap year only if divisible by <b>400</b> (1600, 2000 are leap; 1900 is NOT a leap year!).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The hands of a clock coincide (overlap) exactly 22 times in 24 hours (not 24 times!), because between 11 and 1 they coincide only once at 12:00.', 'number': '38.1', 'title': 'Clock Angles & Calendar Mathematics'}, {'content': '• <b>Cube & Dice Opposites Rule:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- On an unfolded standard cross dice: <b>Alternate faces along any straight line are OPPOSITE to each other</b>!<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;- Two opposite faces can NEVER share an edge or vertex, and can NEVER appear simultaneously in any single 2D projection view.<br/><br/>\n\n• <b>Mirror & Water Reflections:</b><br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Mirror Image:</b> Left and Right swap; Top and Bottom remain unchanged.<br/>\n&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Water Reflection:</b> Top and Bottom swap; Left and Right remain unchanged.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In spatial paper folding questions: Mentally trace the folds in REVERSE order. Each unfolding step creates a symmetric reflection across the crease line.', 'number': '38.2', 'title': 'Spatial Aptitude: 2D/3D Rotations, Paper Folding & Unfolding'}], 'real_life_examples': [{'text': 'Spatial aptitude tests the mechanical engineering ability to visualize 3D mechanical components from top, front, and side 2D technical drawings.', 'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: 3D CAD Modeling & Orthographic Views'}], 'title': 'Analytical & Spatial Aptitude - Logic, Reasoning & 2D/3D Shapes', 'worked_examples': [{'answer': 'Angle is 130°.', 'problem': 'Find the angle between the hands of a clock at 3:40.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify values: H = 3, M = 40.', 'Step 2: Apply clock angle formula: θ = | 30H - (11 / 2)M |.', 'Step 3: Substitute: θ = | 30(3) - (11 / 2)(40) | = | 90 - 220 | = | -130 | = 130°.', 'Step 4: Check if reflex angle is asked. Normal angle = 130°, Reflex angle = 360° - 130° = 230°.'], 'title': 'Worked Example: Angle Between Clock Hands'}]}}
